var tafsirData_25 = {
  "1": "\"Blessed is the One Who sent down the Standard to His servant, so that he may be a warner to the whole world\" opens the chapter of the Standard, and the opening is the blessing, and the blessing is the verse's content. The verse begins with the blessing: \"Blessed is the One Who sent down the Standard to His servant,\" and the word \"Blessed\" is 'tabaraka,' the word of the abounding good, and the word \"Standard\" is 'al-Furqan,' the chapter's own name, and the commentators explain the meaning: the Furqan is the Quran, and it is called the Standard because it separates the truth from the falsehood, and the verse says the Standard was sent down, and the blessing is the verse's first stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's teaching on the Book, and the teaching is that the Book is the blessing of all blessings, and the verse then names the receiver: \"to His servant,\" and the word \"servant\" is 'abdih,' and the verse names Muhammad the servant, and the commentators note the honour folded into the title: the highest rank the Quran gives its Prophet is the servanthood, and the noting is the verse's second stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's teaching on the rank, and the teaching is that the servanthood is the crown, and the verse then states the purpose: \"so that he may be a warner to the whole world\", and the word \"whole world\" is 'alamin,' and the verse says the warning is for all the worlds, and the purpose is the verse's last stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's teaching on the message, and the teaching is that the Standard is the warning for all of humanity, and the teaching is the chapter's opening, and the opening is the next verses' subject, and the subject is the ownership, and the ownership is the chapter's next teaching.",
  "2": "\"˹Allah is˺ the One to Whom belongs the kingdom of the heavens and the earth, Who has never had ˹any˺ offspring, nor does He have a partner in ˹governing˺ the kingdom. He has created everything, ordaining it precisely\" states the four qualities of the Blessed One, and the verse is the teaching on the ownership, and the teaching is the verse's content. The verse states the first quality: \"˹Allah is˺ the One to Whom belongs the kingdom of the heavens and the earth,\" and the word \"kingdom\" is 'mulk,' and the verse says the kingdom is God's, and the saying is the verse's first stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's teaching on the possession, and the teaching is that the ownership is the ground of the warning of the previous verse, and the verse then states the second: \"Who has never had ˹any˺ offspring,\" and the verse denies the child, and the commentators explain the meaning: the denial of the offspring is the chapter's answer to the claims, and the denial is the verse's second stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's teaching on the purity, and the verse then states the third: \"nor does He have a partner in ˹governing˺ the kingdom\", and the verse denies the partner, and the denial is the verse's third stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's teaching on the oneness, and the teaching is that the kingdom has one King, and the verse then states the fourth: \"He has created everything, ordaining it precisely\", and the word \"ordaining\" is 'qaddarahu taqdiran,' the word of the measuring, and the verse says every created thing is measured, and the measuring is the verse's last stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's teaching on the order, and the teaching is that the creation is the measured work of the one King, and the four qualities together are the chapter's creed, and the creed is the next verse's subject, and the subject is the idols, and the idols are the chapter's next teaching.",
  "3": "\"Yet they have taken besides Him gods who cannot create anything but are themselves created. Nor can they protect or benefit themselves. Nor can they control life, death, or resurrection\" states the verdict on the idols, and the verse is the teaching on the false gods, and the teaching is the verse's content. The verse states the taking: \"Yet they have taken besides Him gods who cannot create anything but are themselves created\", and the word \"yet\" is the verse's contrast, and the verse says the taken gods are the created, and the commentators explain the meaning: the idol that is made by the hands is the made, and the making is the verse's first stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's teaching on the absurdity, and the teaching is that the worshipper worships his own work, and the verse then states the two failures: \"Nor can they protect or benefit themselves\", and the verse says the gods cannot guard or give, and the failures are the verse's second stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's teaching on the powerlessness, and the teaching is that the god who cannot help himself cannot help, and the verse then states the three lacks: \"Nor can they control life, death, or resurrection\", and the verse names the three, and the three are the verse's last stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's teaching on the final test, and the teaching is that the god of the idolater has no share in the life, the death, or the rising, and the three lacks are the chapter's answer to the idolatry, and the answer is the next verse's subject, and the subject is the accusation, and the accusation is the chapter's next teaching. The verse is the chapter's teaching on the absurdity, and the absurdity is the worshipper and the made, and the made is the chapter's word on the idol, and the word is that the worshipper worships his own work, and the working is the chapter's answer to the idolatry, and the answer is the next verse's subject, and the subject is the accusation, and the accusation is the chapter's next teaching.",
  "4": "\"The disbelievers say, \"This ˹Quran˺ is nothing but a fabrication which he made up with the help of others.\" Their claim is totally unjustified and untrue\" quotes the accusation, and the verse is the teaching on the slander of the Book, and the teaching is the verse's content. The verse quotes the accusation: \"The disbelievers say, \"This ˹Quran˺ is nothing but a fabrication which he made up with the help of others,\"\" and the word \"fabrication\" is 'ifk,' and the word \"help\" is 'a'anahu,' and the verse records the claim of the human assistance, and the commentators explain the background: the disbelievers of Mecca claimed that the Prophet was taught by human helpers, and the claim is the verse's first stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's teaching on the accusation, and the teaching is that the accusation of the human source is the oldest of the slanders, and the same claim is answered elsewhere: \"And We surely know that they say, \"No one is teaching him except a human,\"\" and the verse of the chapter keeps the accusation, and the keeping is the chapter's design, and the verse then states the verdict: \"Their claim is totally unjustified and untrue,\" and the word \"unjustified\" is 'zulman,' the word of the wrong, and the word \"untrue\" is 'zuran,' the word of the falsehood, and the verdict is the verse's last stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's teaching on the answer, and the teaching is that the claim is the wrong and the lie together, and the two are the chapter's word, and the word is the next verse's subject, and the subject is the fables, and the fables are the chapter's next teaching. The verse is the chapter's teaching on the slander, and the teaching is the claim, and the claim is the chapter's word on the accusation, and the word is that the accusation of the human source is answered by the knowledge of the secrets, and the answering is the next verse's subject, and the subject is the fables, and the fables are the chapter's next teaching.",
  "5": "\"And they say, \"˹These revelations are only˺ ancient fables which he has had written down, and they are rehearsed to him morning and evening\"\" quotes the second accusation, and the verse is the teaching on the old slander, and the teaching is the verse's content. The verse quotes the accusation: \"And they say, \"˹These revelations are only˺ ancient fables which he has had written down, and they are rehearsed to him morning and evening\",\" and the word \"fables\" is 'asatir,' and the verse records the claim of the copying, and the commentators explain the meaning: the disbelievers claimed the Quran was the old tales written by the scribes and rehearsed to the Prophet, and the claim is the verse's first stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's teaching on the accusation, and the teaching is that the claim of the copying contradicts itself, and the verse notes the contradiction, and the noting is the verse's second stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's teaching on the slander's logic, and the teaching is that the man who cannot read is accused of the reading, and the contradiction is the verse's last stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's answer to the accusation, and the answer is the next verse's subject, and the subject is the answer of the revelation, and the answer is the chapter's next teaching. The verse is the chapter's teaching on the slander's logic, and the teaching is the contradiction, and the contradiction is the chapter's word on the accusation, and the word is that the man who cannot read is accused of the reading, and the accusing is the chapter's answer to the claim, and the answer is the next verse's subject, and the subject is the source, and the source is the chapter's next teaching. The verse is the chapter's teaching on the contradiction, and the contradiction is the reading, and the reading is the chapter's word on the slander, and the word is that the man who cannot read is accused of the reading, and the accusing is the chapter's answer to the claim, and the answer is the chapter's word.",
  "6": "\"Say, ˹O Prophet,˺ \"This ˹Quran˺ has been revealed by the One Who knows the secrets of the heavens and the earth. Surely He is All-Forgiving, Most Merciful\"\" states the answer to the accusations, and the verse is the teaching on the source, and the teaching is the verse's content. The verse quotes the answer: \"Say, ˹O Prophet,˺ \"This ˹Quran˺ has been revealed by the One Who knows the secrets of the heavens and the earth\",\" and the word \"secrets\" is 'sirr,' and the verse says the Quran is from the One who knows the secrets, and the commentators explain the meaning: the Book that tells the unseen cannot be the work of the human helpers, and the saying is the verse's first stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's teaching on the proof, and the teaching is that the knowledge of the secrets is the Quran's witness, and the verse then closes with the names: \"Surely He is All-Forgiving, Most Merciful\", and the two names are the verse's second stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's teaching on the invitation, and the teaching is that the answer to the slander ends with the door of the forgiveness, and the door is the verse's last stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's teaching on the manner, and the teaching is that the Book answers the accusation with the truth and the mercy together, and the two are the chapter's word, and the word is the next verse's subject, and the subject is the mockery, and the mockery is the chapter's next teaching. The verse is the chapter's teaching on the answer, and the teaching is the source, and the source is the chapter's word on the accusation, and the word is that the Book that tells the unseen cannot be the human work, and the working is the chapter's answer to the slander, and the answer is the next verse's subject, and the subject is the mockery, and the mockery is the chapter's next teaching.",
  "7": "\"And they say ˹mockingly˺, \"What kind of messenger is this who eats food and goes about in market-places ˹for a living˺? If only an angel had been sent down with him to be his co-warner\"\" quotes the mockery, and the verse is the teaching on the old objection, and the teaching is the verse's content. The verse quotes the mockery: \"And they say ˹mockingly˺, \"What kind of messenger is this who eats food and goes about in market-places ˹for a living˺?\"\" and the verse records the two objections, the eating and the marketing, and the commentators explain the meaning: the disbelievers measured the messenger by the ordinary life, and the measuring is the verse's first stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's teaching on the objection, and the teaching is that the objection to the human messenger is the oldest of the denials, and the same objection is answered elsewhere: \"We did not send ˹messengers˺ before you ˹O Prophet˺ except mere men inspired by Us\", and the verse of the chapter keeps the objection, and the keeping is the chapter's design, and the verse then quotes the demand: \"If only an angel had been sent down with him to be his co-warner\", and the demand is the verse's second stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's teaching on the wish, and the teaching is that the demand for the angel is the demand for the messenger who is not the example, and the verse then leaves the demand to the next verse, and the leaving is the verse's last stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's design, and the design is the next verse's subject, and the subject is the treasure, and the treasure is the chapter's next teaching. The verse is the chapter's teaching on the objection, and the teaching is the humanity, and the humanity is the chapter's word on the messenger, and the word is that the human messenger is the example, and the example is the chapter's answer to the demand for the angel, and the answer is the next verse's subject, and the subject is the treasure, and the treasure is the chapter's next teaching.",
  "8": "\"or a treasure had been cast down to him, or he had had a garden from which he may eat!\" And the wrongdoers say ˹to the believers˺, \"You are only following a bewitched man\"\" completes the mockery, and the verse is the teaching on the demands, and the teaching is the verse's content. The verse completes the list: \"or a treasure had been cast down to him, or he had had a garden from which he may eat!\" and the verse records the two demands, the treasure and the garden, and the commentators explain the meaning: the disbelievers demanded the signs of the wealth, and the demands are the verse's first stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's teaching on the measure, and the teaching is that the deniers measure the messenger by the wealth, and the verse then quotes the second mockery: \"And the wrongdoers say ˹to the believers˺, \"You are only following a bewitched man,\"\" and the word \"bewitched\" is 'mashuran,' and the verse records the accusation of the bewitchment, and the commentators explain the meaning: the accusation of the magic is the denial's last weapon, and the accusation is the verse's second stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's teaching on the slander, and the teaching is that the bewitchment is the oldest name the deniers give the truth, and the verse then closes, and the closing is the verse's last stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's answer to the demands, and the answer is the next verse's subject, and the subject is the naming, and the naming is the chapter's next teaching. The verse is the chapter's teaching on the demands, and the teaching is the wealth, and the wealth is the chapter's word on the measure, and the word is that the deniers measure the messenger by the wealth, and the measuring is the chapter's answer to the mockery, and the answer is the next verse's subject, and the subject is the naming, and the naming is the chapter's next teaching.",
  "9": "\"See ˹O Prophet˺ how they call you names! So they have gone so ˹far˺ astray that they cannot find the ˹Right˺ Way\" states the chapter's comment on the names, and the verse is the teaching on the slander, and the teaching is the verse's content. The verse begins with the comment: \"See ˹O Prophet˺ how they call you names!\" and the word \"names\" is 'amtal,' the word of the parables, and the verse points to the names the previous verses recorded, and the commentators explain the meaning: the names were the slanders, and the verse says the slanderers have no way, and the saying is the verse's first stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's teaching on the slander, and the teaching is that the name-calling is the slanderer's whole stock, and the verse then states the verdict: \"So they have gone so ˹far˺ astray that they cannot find the ˹Right˺ Way\", and the word \"astray\" is 'dallu,' and the verse says the straying is so far that the way is lost, and the verdict is the verse's last stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's teaching on the end, and the teaching is that the slanderer loses the way by his slander, and the teaching is the chapter's word, and the word is the next verse's subject, and the subject is the answer, and the answer is the chapter's next teaching. The verse is the chapter's teaching on the slander, and the teaching is the naming, and the naming is the chapter's word on the slanderer, and the word is that the slanderer loses the way by his slander, and the losing is the chapter's answer to the names, and the answer is the next verse's subject, and the subject is the better, and the better is the chapter's next teaching.",
  "10": "\"Blessed is the One Who—if He wills—can give you far better than ˹all˺ that: gardens under which rivers flow, and palaces as well\" answers the demands, and the verse is the teaching on the answer, and the teaching is the verse's content. The verse states the answer: \"Blessed is the One Who—if He wills—can give you far better than ˹all˺ that: gardens under which rivers flow, and palaces as well\", and the word \"better\" is 'khayr,' and the verse says the treasure and the garden they demanded are small beside what God can give, and the commentators explain the meaning: the answer does not deny the giving but raises it, and the raising is the verse's first stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's teaching on the answer, and the teaching is that the Prophet's wealth is not of this world, and the verse then notes the word \"if He wills\", and the noting is the verse's second stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's teaching on the will, and the teaching is that the giving is the will's, and the verse then closes, and the closing is the verse's last stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's answer to the mockers, and the answer is the better, and the better is the chapter's word, and the word is the next verse's subject, and the subject is the Hour, and the Hour is the chapter's next teaching. The verse is the chapter's teaching on the answer, and the teaching is the better, and the better is the chapter's word on the demands, and the word is that the Prophet's wealth is not of this world, and the wealth is the chapter's answer to the mockers, and the answer is the next verse's subject, and the subject is the Hour, and the Hour is the chapter's next teaching.",
  "11": "\"In fact, they deny the Hour. And for the deniers of the Hour, We have prepared a blazing Fire\" states the root of the denial, and the verse is the teaching on the Hour, and the teaching is the verse's content. The verse states the root: \"In fact, they deny the Hour\", and the word \"in fact\" is the verse's correction, and the verse says the denial of the Hour is the root of every demand, and the commentators explain the meaning: the one who denies the Hour demands the treasure and the garden because he has no horizon beyond the world, and the saying is the verse's first stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's teaching on the root, and the teaching is that the denial of the Hour explains the mockery, and the verse then states the end: \"And for the deniers of the Hour, We have prepared a blazing Fire\", and the word \"blazing\" is 'sa'ir,' and the verse says the prepared is the Fire, and the end is the verse's last stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's teaching on the consequence, and the teaching is that the denier of the Hour meets the Hour, and the meeting is the chapter's word, and the word is the next verse's subject, and the subject is the sight, and the sight is the chapter's next teaching. The verse is the chapter's teaching on the root, and the teaching is the Hour, and the Hour is the chapter's word on the denial, and the word is that the denial of the Hour explains the mockery, and the explaining is the chapter's answer to the demands, and the answer is the next verse's subject, and the subject is the sight, and the sight is the chapter's next teaching. The verse is the chapter's teaching on the root, and the root is the Hour, and the Hour is the chapter's word on the denial, and the word is that the denial of the Hour explains the mockery, and the explaining is the chapter's answer to the demands, and the answer is the chapter's word.",
  "12": "\"Once it sees them from a distance, they will hear it fuming and growling\" opens the description of the Fire, and the verse is the teaching on the approach, and the teaching is the verse's content. The verse states the approach: \"Once it sees them from a distance, they will hear it fuming and growling\", and the word \"sees\" is 'ra'athum,' and the verse gives the Fire the seeing, and the commentators explain the meaning: the Fire is described as if it sees its people, and the description is the verse's first stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's teaching on the terror, and the teaching is that the Fire itself is the pursuer, and the verse then states the sound: \"they will hear it fuming and growling\", and the word \"fuming\" is 'taghayyuz,' the word of the boiling rage, and the word \"growling\" is 'zafir,' and the verse says the Fire's rage is heard from the distance, and the sound is the verse's last stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's teaching on the nearness, and the teaching is that the end is heard before it is felt, and the teaching is the chapter's word, and the word is the next verse's subject, and the subject is the narrow place, and the narrow place is the chapter's next teaching. The verse is the chapter's teaching on the approach, and the teaching is the sound, and the sound is the chapter's word on the Fire, and the word is that the end is heard before it is felt, and the hearing is the chapter's answer to the mockery, and the answer is the next verse's subject, and the subject is the narrow place, and the narrow place is the chapter's next teaching. The verse is the chapter's teaching on the approach, and the approach is the sound, and the sound is the chapter's word on the Fire, and the word is that the end is heard before it is felt, and the hearing is the chapter's answer to the mockery, and the answer is the chapter's word.",
  "13": "\"And when they are tossed into a narrow place inside ˹Hell˺, chained together, then and there they will cry out for ˹instant˺ destruction\" completes the description, and the verse is the teaching on the narrowness, and the teaching is the verse's content. The verse states the tossing: \"And when they are tossed into a narrow place inside ˹Hell˺, chained together,\" and the word \"tossed\" is 'ulqu,' and the word \"narrow\" is 'dayyiqan,' and the verse says the place is narrow and the people are chained, and the commentators explain the meaning: the narrowness is the punishment's shape, and the chains are the joining, and the two are the verse's first stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's teaching on the confinement, and the teaching is that the people of the Fire are pressed and joined, and the verse then states the cry: \"then and there they will cry out for ˹instant˺ destruction\", and the word \"destruction\" is 'thuburan,' and the verse says the cry is for the instant end, and the commentators explain the meaning: the cry for the death is the last hope, and the verse says the hope is the cry, and the cry is the verse's last stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's teaching on the end, and the teaching is that the narrow place answers the wide mockery, and the teaching is the chapter's word, and the word is the next verses' subject, and the subject is the answer to the cry, and the answer is the chapter's next teaching. The verse is the chapter's teaching on the confinement, and the teaching is the narrowness, and the narrowness is the chapter's word on the end, and the word is that the narrow place answers the wide mockery, and the answering is the chapter's answer to the deniers, and the answer is the next verse's subject, and the subject is the cry's answer, and the answer is the chapter's next teaching.",
  "14": "\"˹They will be told,˺ \"Do not cry only once for destruction, but cry many times over!\"\" answers the cry of the previous verse, and the verse is the teaching on the hopelessness, and the teaching is the verse's content. The verse quotes the answer: \"˹They will be told,˺ \"Do not cry only once for destruction, but cry many times over!\"\" and the verse answers the cry for the instant destruction with the command to cry again, and the commentators explain the meaning: the destruction they cry for will not come, and the crying itself is multiplied, and the multiplication is the verse's first stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's teaching on the closed door, and the teaching is that the cry of the Fire's people is answered with the doubling of the cry, and the verse then leaves the scene, and the leaving is the verse's last stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's design, and the design is the next verse's subject, and the subject is the comparison, and the comparison is the chapter's next teaching. The verse is the chapter's closing of the scene that began with the tossed and the chained, and the closing is the answer, and the answer is the doubled cry, and the doubled cry is the chapter's word on the hopelessness, and the word is that the cry for the end is itself multiplied, and the multiplication is the chapter's teaching that the Fire has no exit, and the teaching stands behind the comparison that the next verse opens, and the comparison is the chapter's next stroke in the argument. The answer is the chapter's seal on the scene, and the seal is the doubled cry, and the doubled cry is the chapter's final word on the Fire's people, and the word is that their only asking is met with the multiplication of the asking, and the multiplication is the chapter's teaching that the end they seek is not granted, and the teaching closes the scene and opens the comparison.",
  "15": "\"Say, ˹O Prophet,˺ \"Is this better or the Garden of Eternity which the righteous have been promised, as a reward and ˹an ultimate˺ destination\"\" opens the comparison, and the verse is the teaching on the choice, and the teaching is the verse's content. The verse quotes the question: \"Say, ˹O Prophet,˺ \"Is this better or the Garden of Eternity which the righteous have been promised, as a reward and ˹an ultimate˺ destination\"\" and the word \"this\" points to the Fire of the previous verses, and the verse sets the Fire and the Garden side by side, and the commentators explain the meaning: the question is the chapter's method of the comparison, and the method is the verse's first stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's teaching on the choice, and the teaching is that the two ends are shown and the hearer chooses, and the verse then names the Garden's people: \"which the righteous have been promised,\" and the word \"righteous\" is 'muttaqin,' and the verse says the Garden is the promised of the God-fearing, and the naming is the verse's second stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's teaching on the condition, and the teaching is that the Garden is the promise to the God-fearing, and the verse then names the promise's two faces: \"as a reward and ˹an ultimate˺ destination\", and the two faces are the verse's last stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's teaching on the end, and the teaching is that the Garden is both the wage and the home, and the teaching is the chapter's word, and the word is the next verse's subject, and the subject is the wish, and the wish is the chapter's next teaching. The verse is the chapter's turning of the question back on the hearer, and the turning is the method, and the method is the comparison, and the comparison is the chapter's teaching on the clarity, and the teaching is that the two ends are not hidden, and the one who chooses the Fire over the Garden has chosen with open eyes, and the eyes are the chapter's gift to the hearer.",
  "16": "\"There they will have whatever they wish for, forever. That is a promise ˹to be sought after˺, binding on your Lord\" describes the Garden, and the verse is the teaching on the wish, and the teaching is the verse's content. The verse states the gift: \"There they will have whatever they wish for, forever\", and the word \"whatever\" is the verse's wideness, and the word \"forever\" is 'khalidin,' and the verse says the wish and the permanence are joined, and the commentators explain the meaning: the Garden's gift is the satisfied wish, and the satisfaction is the verse's first stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's teaching on the Garden, and the teaching is that the Garden is the wish fulfilled without end, and the verse then states the guarantee: \"That is a promise ˹to be sought after˺, binding on your Lord\", and the word \"binding\" is 'mas'ulan,' the word of the asked, and the verse says the promise is the obligation God has placed on Himself, and the commentators explain the meaning: the promise is asked after, and the asking is the verse's second stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's teaching on the certainty, and the teaching is that the promise of the Garden is the binding word of the Lord, and the verse then closes, and the closing is the verse's last stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's answer to the doubt, and the answer is the next verse's subject, and the subject is the gathering, and the gathering is the chapter's next teaching. The verse is the chapter's word on the Garden's law, and the word is the wish, and the wish is the chapter's teaching on the generosity, and the teaching is that the Garden is not the measured wage but the open wish, and the openness is the chapter's answer to the fear of the lack, and the answer is the binding promise, and the binding is the chapter's seal on the whole promise.",
  "17": "\"˹Watch for˺ the Day He will gather them along with what they used to worship besides Allah, and ask ˹the objects of worship˺, \"Was it you who misled these servants of Mine, or did they stray from the Way ˹on their own˺?\"\" opens the scene of the gathering, and the verse is the teaching on the Day's question, and the teaching is the verse's content. The verse states the gathering: \"˹Watch for˺ the Day He will gather them along with what they used to worship besides Allah,\" and the word \"gather\" is 'yahshuruhum,' and the verse says the worshippers and the worshipped are gathered together, and the commentators explain the meaning: the idols and their worshippers stand in one gathering, and the gathering is the verse's first stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's teaching on the Day, and the teaching is that the Day gathers the two sides of the worship, and the verse then states the question: \"and ask ˹the objects of worship˺, \"Was it you who misled these servants of Mine, or did they stray from the Way ˹on their own˺?\"\" and the question is put to the worshipped, and the commentators explain the meaning: the question exposes the lie of the claim, and the exposure is the verse's second stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's teaching on the justice, and the teaching is that the Day asks before it judges, and the verse then leaves the question to the next verse, and the leaving is the verse's last stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's design, and the design is the next verse's subject, and the subject is the answer, and the answer is the chapter's next teaching. The verse is the chapter's opening of the Day's court, and the opening is the question, and the question is the chapter's teaching on the justice, and the teaching is that the Day asks before it judges, and the asking is the mercy that exposes the lie without the argument, and the exposure is the chapter's answer to the idolatry, and the answer is the next verse's record of the disowning.",
  "18": "\"They will say, \"Glory be to You! It was not right for ˹others like˺ us to take any lords besides You, but You allowed enjoyment for them and their forefathers ˹for so long˺ that they forgot ˹Your˺ remembrance and became a doomed people\"\" records the answer of the worshipped, and the verse is the teaching on the disowning, and the teaching is the verse's content. The verse quotes the answer: \"They will say, \"Glory be to You! It was not right for ˹others like˺ us to take any lords besides You,\"\" and the verse records the glorification, and the commentators explain the meaning: the worshipped begin with the declaring of God's purity, and the beginning is the verse's first stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's teaching on the Day's manner, and the teaching is that even the false gods glorify on the Day, and the verse then records the disowning: \"but You allowed enjoyment for them and their forefathers ˹for so long˺ that they forgot ˹Your˺ remembrance and became a doomed people\", and the verse says the worshipped disown the worshippers, and the commentators explain the meaning: the idols place the blame on the worshippers' forgetfulness, and the disowning is the verse's second stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's teaching on the exposure, and the teaching is that the Day tears the bond of the worship, and the verse then closes, and the closing is the verse's last stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's answer to the idolatry, and the answer is the next verse's subject, and the subject is the verdict, and the verdict is the chapter's next teaching. The verse is the chapter's record of the Day's first answer, and the record is the glorification, and the glorification is the chapter's teaching on the Day's manner, and the teaching is that even the false gods declare the purity on the Day, and the declaring is the chapter's exposure of the world's lie, and the exposure is the chapter's answer to the idolater's hope, and the answer is that the hoped intercessor is the first to flee.",
  "19": "\"˹The doomed will be told˺, \"Your gods have clearly denied your claims. So now you can neither ward off ˹the punishment˺ nor get any help.\" And whoever of you does wrong, We will make them taste a horrible punishment\" states the verdict on the worshippers, and the verse is the teaching on the abandonment, and the teaching is the verse's content. The verse quotes the verdict: \"˹The doomed will be told˺, \"Your gods have clearly denied your claims,\"\" and the word \"denied\" is 'kadhdhabukum,' and the verse says the gods have denied their worshippers, and the commentators explain the meaning: the worshipped disowned the worshippers, and the disowning is the verdict, and the verse then states the consequence: \"So now you can neither ward off ˹the punishment˺ nor get any help\", and the verse closes the two exits, the warding and the helping, and the closing is the verse's first stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's teaching on the end, and the teaching is that the idolater stands alone, and the verse then states the law: \"And whoever of you does wrong, We will make them taste a horrible punishment\", and the word \"taste\" is 'nudhiqhu,' and the verse says the wrongdoer tastes the horrible, and the law is the verse's last stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's teaching on the justice, and the teaching is that the punishment follows the wrong, and the teaching is the chapter's word, and the word is the next verse's subject, and the subject is the messengers, and the messengers are the chapter's next teaching. The verse is the chapter's verdict on the worshippers, and the verdict is the abandonment, and the abandonment is the chapter's teaching on the end of the false reliance, and the teaching is that the relied-upon disowns the reliant, and the disowning is the chapter's answer to every reliance that is not on God, and the answer is the taste of the horrible, and the taste is the chapter's seal on the wrong.",
  "20": "\"We never sent any messenger before you ˹O Prophet˺, who did not eat food and go about in market-places. We have made some of you a trial for others. Will you ˹not then˺ be patient? And your Lord is All-Seeing\" answers the objection of the human messenger, and the verse is the teaching on the pattern, and the teaching is the verse's content. The verse states the pattern: \"We never sent any messenger before you ˹O Prophet˺, who did not eat food and go about in market-places\", and the verse says every messenger ate and walked the markets, and the commentators explain the meaning: the objection of the previous verses was answered by the pattern of all the messengers, and the pattern is the verse's first stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's teaching on the message, and the teaching is that the human messenger is the design, and the verse then states the trial: \"We have made some of you a trial for others\", and the word \"trial\" is 'fitnah,' and the verse says the people are the tests of one another, and the commentators explain the meaning: the poor believer is the trial of the rich denier, and the trial is the verse's second stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's teaching on the arrangement, and the teaching is that the ranks of the world are the tests, and the verse then asks: \"Will you ˹not then˺ be patient?\" and the question is the verse's third stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's teaching on the response, and the teaching is that the trial's answer is the patience, and the verse then closes: \"And your Lord is All-Seeing\", and the closing is the verse's last stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's teaching on the awareness, and the teaching is that the patience is seen, and the teaching is the chapter's word, and the word is the next verse's subject, and the subject is the demand, and the demand is the chapter's next teaching.",
  "21": "\"Those who do not expect to meet Us say, \"If only the angels were sent down to us, or we could see our Lord!\" They have certainly been carried away by their arrogance and have entirely exceeded all limits\" quotes the demand of the deniers, and the verse is the teaching on the arrogance, and the teaching is the verse's content. The verse quotes the demand: \"Those who do not expect to meet Us say, \"If only the angels were sent down to us, or we could see our Lord!\"\" and the verse names the speakers first, and the commentators explain the meaning: the demand comes from the ones who do not expect the meeting, and the naming is the verse's first stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's teaching on the root, and the teaching is that the demand is the fruit of the denial of the meeting, and the verse then states the verdict: \"They have certainly been carried away by their arrogance and have entirely exceeded all limits\", and the word \"arrogance\" is 'istakbaru,' and the word \"exceeded\" is 'ataw,' and the verse says the demand is the arrogance, and the verdict is the verse's last stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's teaching on the demand, and the teaching is that the demand to see God is the arrogance of the one who has not prepared, and the teaching is the chapter's word, and the word is the next verse's subject, and the subject is the Day, and the Day is the chapter's next teaching. The verse is the chapter's record of the demand, and the record is the naming of the demanders, and the naming is the chapter's teaching on the root, and the teaching is that the demand for the sight is the fruit of the denial of the meeting, and the fruit is the chapter's answer to the demand, and the answer is that the demanded sight is the unprepared's terror, and the terror is the next verse's scene.",
  "22": "\"˹But˺ on the Day they will see the angels, there will be no good news for the wicked, who will cry, \"Keep away! Away ˹from us˺!\"\" answers the demand, and the verse is the teaching on the meeting, and the teaching is the verse's content. The verse states the meeting: \"˹But˺ on the Day they will see the angels, there will be no good news for the wicked,\" and the verse says the demanded sight will come, and the verse says the sight is the bad news, and the commentators explain the meaning: the angels they demanded in the world will be seen on the Day, and the seeing is the punishment's beginning, and the saying is the verse's first stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's teaching on the irony, and the teaching is that the demanded angel is the dreaded angel, and the verse then quotes the cry: \"who will cry, \"Keep away! Away ˹from us˺!\"\" and the word \"keep away\" is 'hijran mahjuran,' and the verse says the wicked will cry for the separation, and the commentators explain the meaning: the ones who asked for the angels will flee from the angels, and the crying is the verse's last stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's teaching on the reversal, and the teaching is that the Day reverses the demands, and the teaching is the chapter's word, and the word is the next verse's subject, and the subject is the deeds, and the deeds are the chapter's next teaching. The verse is the chapter's reversal of the demand, and the reversal is the irony, and the irony is the chapter's teaching on the Day, and the teaching is that the Day turns the wished into the feared, and the turning is the chapter's answer to the mockers, and the answer is the cry for the separation, and the cry is the chapter's record of the reversal's depth.",
  "23": "\"Then We will turn to whatever ˹good˺ deeds they did, reducing them to scattered dust\" states the end of the deeds, and the verse is the teaching on the empty deed, and the teaching is the verse's content. The verse states the turning: \"Then We will turn to whatever ˹good˺ deeds they did, reducing them to scattered dust\", and the word \"dust\" is 'haba'an manthuran,' the word of the scattered motes, and the verse says the deeds are turned to the dust, and the commentators explain the meaning: the good deeds of the one who denied the meeting are scattered like the motes in the air, and the scattering is the verse's first stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's teaching on the condition, and the teaching is that the deed without the faith is the mote, and the same teaching is stated elsewhere: \"The parable of the deeds of those who disbelieve in their Lord is that of ashes fiercely blown away by wind on a stormy day\", and the verse of the chapter keeps the dust, and the keeping is the chapter's design, and the verse then closes, and the closing is the verse's last stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's answer to the denier's deed, and the answer is the next verse's subject, and the subject is the Garden's people, and the people are the chapter's next teaching. The verse is the chapter's word on the empty deed, and the word is the dust, and the dust is the chapter's teaching on the condition, and the teaching is that the deed without the faith is the mote, and the mote is the chapter's answer to the denier's account, and the answer is the scattering, and the scattering is the chapter's picture of the loss that no deed can carry.",
  "24": "\"˹But˺ on that Day the residents of Paradise will have the best settlement and the finest place to rest\" contrasts the scattered deeds with the Garden's people, and the verse is the teaching on the settlement, and the teaching is the verse's content. The verse states the contrast: \"˹But˺ on that Day the residents of Paradise will have the best settlement and the finest place to rest\", and the word \"settlement\" is 'mustaqarr,' and the word \"rest\" is 'maqil,' and the verse names the two, and the commentators explain the meaning: the settlement is the staying, and the rest is the noonday resting, and the verse says the Garden's people have the best of both, and the contrast is the verse's first stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's teaching on the Day's division, and the teaching is that the same Day that scatters the deeds settles the believers, and the verse then closes, and the closing is the verse's last stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's answer to the wicked's cry, and the answer is the two ends, and the two ends are the next verse's subject, and the subject is the bursting, and the bursting is the chapter's next teaching. The verse is the chapter's contrast to the scattering, and the contrast is the settlement, and the settlement is the chapter's teaching on the Day's division, and the teaching is that the same Day that scatters the deeds settles the believers, and the settling is the chapter's answer to the wicked's cry, and the answer is the two ends, and the two ends are the chapter's picture of the Day's justice. The verse is the chapter's short picture of the Day's two sides, and the picture is the settlement against the scattering, and the two are the chapter's teaching on the justice, and the teaching is that the Day gives to each what they built, and the building is the chapter's word on the two lives, and the word stands behind the scene of the heavens that follows.",
  "25": "\"˹Watch for˺ the Day the heavens will burst with clouds, and the angels will be sent down in successive ranks\" opens the scene of the heavens, and the verse is the teaching on the bursting, and the teaching is the verse's content. The verse states the bursting: \"˹Watch for˺ the Day the heavens will burst with clouds, and the angels will be sent down in successive ranks\", and the word \"burst\" is 'tashaqqaqu,' the word of the splitting, and the verse says the heavens split with the clouds, and the commentators explain the meaning: the sky that sheltered the world opens on the Day, and the angels descend in the ranks, and the two together are the verse's first stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's teaching on the Day, and the teaching is that the Day is the opening of the heavens and the descending of the angels, and the verse then closes, and the closing is the verse's last stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's answer to the demand for the angels, and the answer is the descent, and the descent is the next verse's subject, and the subject is the authority, and the authority is the chapter's next teaching. The verse is the chapter's scene of the heavens, and the scene is the bursting, and the bursting is the chapter's teaching on the Day, and the teaching is that the sky that sheltered the world opens, and the opening is the chapter's answer to the demand for the angels, and the answer is the descent, and the descent is the chapter's picture of the Day's beginning. The verse is the chapter's picture of the sky's end, and the picture is the bursting, and the bursting is the chapter's teaching on the world's closing, and the teaching is that the shelter is temporary, and the temporary is the chapter's answer to the one who trusts the sky, and the answer is the descending ranks, and the ranks are the chapter's picture of the Day's arrival.",
  "26": "\"True authority on that Day will belong ˹only˺ to the Most Compassionate. And it will be a hard day for the disbelievers\" states the authority of the Day, and the verse is the teaching on the kingship, and the teaching is the verse's content. The verse states the authority: \"True authority on that Day will belong ˹only˺ to the Most Compassionate\", and the word \"true\" is 'al-haqq,' and the verse says the authority of the Day is the true, and the commentators explain the meaning: the kings of the world hold the borrowed crowns, and the Day returns the crown to its owner, and the verse names the owner the Most Compassionate, and the naming is the verse's first stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's teaching on the Day's King, and the teaching is that the King of the Day is the Merciful, and the verse then states the result: \"And it will be a hard day for the disbelievers\", and the word \"hard\" is 'asir,' and the verse says the Day is the hard for the disbelievers, and the saying is the verse's last stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's teaching on the division, and the teaching is that the same Day that is the mercy is the hardship, and the teaching is the chapter's word, and the word is the next verse's subject, and the subject is the regret, and the regret is the chapter's next teaching. The verse is the chapter's word on the kingship, and the word is the true, and the true is the chapter's teaching on the Day's King, and the teaching is that the borrowed crowns return to the owner, and the returning is the chapter's answer to the world's kings, and the answer is the hardship, and the hardship is the chapter's picture of the disbeliever's Day.",
  "27": "\"And ˹beware of˺ the Day the wrongdoer will bite his nails ˹in regret˺ and say, \"Oh! I wish I had followed the Way along with the Messenger\"\" opens the scene of the regret, and the verse is the teaching on the biting, and the teaching is the verse's content. The verse states the picture: \"And ˹beware of˺ the Day the wrongdoer will bite his nails ˹in regret˺ and say, \"Oh! I wish I had followed the Way along with the Messenger\",\" and the word \"bite\" is 'ya'addu,' and the verse says the wrongdoer bites his own hands, and the commentators explain the meaning: the biting is the picture of the extreme regret, and the picture is the verse's first stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's teaching on the regret, and the teaching is that the regret of the Day is the biting, and the verse then quotes the wish: \"Oh! I wish I had followed the Way along with the Messenger\", and the wish is the verse's second stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's teaching on the missed, and the teaching is that the wrongdoer wishes the road he left, and the verse then leaves the wish to the next verse, and the leaving is the verse's last stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's design, and the design is the next verse's subject, and the subject is the friend, and the friend is the chapter's next teaching. The verse is the chapter's scene of the regret, and the scene is the biting, and the biting is the chapter's teaching on the regret's depth, and the teaching is that the regret of the Day is physical, and the physical is the chapter's answer to the world's heedlessness, and the answer is the wish, and the wish is the chapter's record of the missed road.",
  "28": "\"Woe to me! I wish I had never taken so-and-so as a close friend\" continues the regret, and the verse is the teaching on the company, and the teaching is the verse's content. The verse quotes the second wish: \"Woe to me! I wish I had never taken so-and-so as a close friend\", and the word \"so-and-so\" is 'fulan,' the word of the unnamed, and the verse says the regretted one is the friend, and the commentators explain the meaning: the wrongdoer regrets the company that led him, and the naming of the friend is the verse's first stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's teaching on the company, and the teaching is that the friend of the world is the regret of the Day, and the verse then leaves the friend unnamed, and the leaving is the verse's second stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's teaching on the commonness, and the teaching is that the regretted friend is every friend of the evil, and the verse then closes, and the closing is the verse's last stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's answer to the wish of the previous verse, and the answer is the next verse's subject, and the subject is the straying, and the straying is the chapter's next teaching. The verse is the chapter's word on the company, and the word is the friend, and the friend is the chapter's teaching on the company's weight, and the teaching is that the friend of the world is the regret of the Day, and the regret is the chapter's answer to the careless companionship, and the answer is the unnamed friend, and the unnamed is the chapter's warning that the regretted friend is every friend of the evil. The verse is the chapter's warning to every reader, and the warning is the friend, and the friend is the chapter's teaching on the choice, and the teaching is that the company is chosen now and regretted then, and the choosing is the chapter's word on the free will, and the word is that the regret does not undo the choice, and the undoing is the next verse's subject.",
  "29": "\"It was he who truly made me stray from the Reminder after it had reached me.\" And Satan has always betrayed humanity\" completes the regret, and the verse is the teaching on the betrayal, and the teaching is the verse's content. The verse quotes the third word: \"It was he who truly made me stray from the Reminder after it had reached me\", and the verse records the blame of the friend, and the commentators explain the meaning: the wrongdoer blames the friend for the straying after the guidance, and the blaming is the verse's first stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's teaching on the blame, and the teaching is that the Day's blame is the world's company, and the verse then states the law: \"And Satan has always betrayed humanity\", and the word \"betrayed\" is 'khadhulan,' the word of the abandoning, and the verse says Satan's way is the abandonment, and the commentators explain the meaning: the tempter walks away from the tempted, and the saying is the verse's last stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's teaching on the enemy, and the teaching is that Satan is the abandoner at the end, and the same scene is described elsewhere: \"But when such a person comes to Us, one will say ˹to their associate˺, \"I wish you were as far from me as the two easts!\"\" and the verse of the chapter keeps the betrayal, and the keeping is the chapter's design, and the design is the next verse's subject, and the subject is the Messenger's cry, and the cry is the chapter's next teaching. The verse is the chapter's teaching on the enemy, and the teaching is the betrayal, and the betrayal is the chapter's word on Satan, and the word is that the tempter walks away from the tempted, and the walking is the chapter's answer to the one who trusts the whisper, and the answer is the abandonment, and the abandonment is the chapter's seal on the enemy's manner.",
  "30": "\"The Messenger has cried, \"O my Lord! My people have indeed received this Quran with neglect\"\" records the Messenger's cry, and the verse is the teaching on the neglect, and the teaching is the verse's content. The verse quotes the cry: \"The Messenger has cried, \"O my Lord! My people have indeed received this Quran with neglect\",\" and the word \"neglect\" is 'mahjuran,' the word of the abandoned, and the verse says the people received the Quran and abandoned it, and the commentators explain the meaning: the neglect is not the absence of the hearing but the abandonment of the acting, and the saying is the verse's first stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's teaching on the neglect, and the teaching is that the abandoned Book is the neglect, and the verse then notes the cry's audience: \"O my Lord!\" and the noting is the verse's second stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's teaching on the complaint, and the teaching is that the Messenger's complaint is carried to the Lord, and the verse then closes, and the closing is the verse's last stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's answer to the neglect, and the answer is the next verse's subject, and the subject is the enemies, and the enemies are the chapter's next teaching. The verse is the chapter's record of the Messenger's complaint, and the record is the neglect, and the neglect is the chapter's teaching on the abandoned Book, and the teaching is that the neglect is the abandonment of the acting, and the abandoning is the chapter's answer to the hearing without the doing, and the answer is the complaint carried to the Lord, and the carrying is the chapter's picture of the Messenger's care for his people even as they neglect. The verse is the chapter's echo of the Prophet's own care, and the echo is the neglect, and the neglect is the chapter's teaching on the Book, and the teaching is that the Book is the trust of the people, and the trusting is the chapter's answer to the one who owns the Book and does not live it, and the answer is the complaint, and the complaint is the chapter's record for every age.",
  "31": "\"Similarly, We made enemies for every prophet from among the wicked, but sufficient is your Lord as a Guide and Helper\" comforts the Messenger, and the verse is the teaching on the pattern, and the teaching is the verse's content. The verse states the pattern: \"Similarly, We made enemies for every prophet from among the wicked,\" and the word \"Similarly\" ties the verse to the cry, and the verse says every prophet had the enemies, and the commentators explain the meaning: the enmity of the wicked is the standing pattern of the prophethood, and the pattern is the verse's first stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's teaching on the comfort, and the teaching is that the Messenger is not the first to face the enemies, and the same pattern is stated elsewhere: \"And so We have made for every prophet enemies—devilish humans and jinn—whispering to one another\", and the verse of the chapter keeps the pattern, and the keeping is the chapter's design, and the verse then states the support: \"but sufficient is your Lord as a Guide and Helper\", and the word \"sufficient\" is 'kafa,' and the verse says the Lord is the enough, and the saying is the verse's last stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's teaching on the sufficiency, and the teaching is that the Guide and the Helper are the one Lord, and the teaching is the chapter's word, and the word is the next verse's subject, and the subject is the staging, and the staging is the chapter's next teaching. The verse is the chapter's comfort to the Messenger, and the comfort is the pattern, and the pattern is the chapter's teaching on the enmity, and the teaching is that the enmity of the wicked is the standing companion of the prophethood, and the companion is the chapter's answer to the loneliness of the caller, and the answer is the sufficiency, and the sufficiency is the chapter's word that the Guide and the Helper are the one Lord.",
  "32": "\"The disbelievers say, \"If only the Quran had been sent down to him all at once!\" ˹We have sent it˺ as such ˹in stages˺ so We may reassure your heart with it. And We have revealed it at a deliberate pace\" answers the objection of the staging, and the verse is the teaching on the gradual, and the teaching is the verse's content. The verse quotes the objection: \"The disbelievers say, \"If only the Quran had been sent down to him all at once!\"\" and the verse records the demand, and the commentators explain the meaning: the deniers demanded the single sending, and the verse then states the wisdom: \"˹We have sent it˺ as such ˹in stages˺ so We may reassure your heart with it. And We have revealed it at a deliberate pace\", and the verse names the two wisdoms, and the two are the verse's heart, and the commentators explain the meaning: the staged sending steadied the Prophet's heart, and the deliberate pace carried the community through the rulings step by step, and the two are the verse's first stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's teaching on the wisdom, and the teaching is that the gradual is the mercy, and the same wisdom is stated elsewhere: \"˹It is˺ a Quran We have revealed in stages so that you may recite it to people at a deliberate pace\", and the verse of the chapter keeps the wisdom, and the keeping is the chapter's design, and the verse then closes, and the closing is the verse's last stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's answer to the objection, and the answer is the next verse's subject, and the subject is the refutation, and the refutation is the chapter's next teaching. The verse is the chapter's answer to the objection, and the answer is the wisdom, and the wisdom is the chapter's teaching on the gradual, and the teaching is that the staged sending steadied the heart and carried the community, and the carrying is the chapter's answer to the demand for the single, and the answer is the deliberate pace, and the pace is the chapter's word on the mercy of the stages.",
  "33": "\"Whenever they bring you an argument, We come to you with the right refutation and the best explanation\" closes the section, and the verse is the teaching on the answer, and the teaching is the verse's content. The verse states the promise: \"Whenever they bring you an argument, We come to you with the right refutation and the best explanation\", and the word \"Whenever\" is the verse's width, and the verse says every argument is answered, and the commentators explain the meaning: the refutation is the truth that breaks the argument, and the explanation is the clarity that settles it, and the two together are the verse's first stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's teaching on the guarantee, and the teaching is that the truth is never left without the answer, and the verse then closes, and the closing is the verse's last stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's answer to the demanders, and the answer is the two gifts, the refutation and the explanation, and the two gifts are the chapter's word, and the word is the next verses' subject, and the subject is the gathering of the deniers, and the gathering is the chapter's next teaching. The verse is the chapter's guarantee to the Messenger, and the guarantee is the answer, and the answer is the chapter's teaching on the truth, and the teaching is that the truth is never left without its reply, and the replying is the chapter's answer to every argument of every age, and the answer is the two gifts, and the two gifts are the chapter's word that the refutation breaks the argument and the explanation settles it, and the settling is the chapter's closing of the objection, and the closing is the chapter's transition to the final scenes. The verse is the chapter's closing of the objections, and the closing is the guarantee, and the guarantee is the chapter's word on the truth, and the word is that the truth has its answer ready, and the readiness is the chapter's teaching on the certainty, and the teaching is the chapter's gift to the Messenger and to every caller, and the gift closes the section and opens the final scenes of the chapter.",
  "34": "\"Those who will be dragged into Hell on their faces will be in the worst place, and are ˹now˺ farthest from the ˹Right˺ Way\" states the chapter's verdict on the mockers, and the verse is the teaching on the dragged, and the teaching is the verse's content. The verse states the picture: \"Those who will be dragged into Hell on their faces\", and the word \"dragged\" is 'yuhsharun,' the word of the gathering, and the verse says the gathering of the mockers is the dragging on the faces, and the commentators explain the meaning: the face that was raised in the mockery is dragged in the dust, and the picture is the verse's first stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's teaching on the reversal, and the teaching is that the mocking face meets the dragging, and the verse then states the two verdicts: \"will be in the worst place, and are ˹now˺ farthest from the ˹Right˺ Way\", and the two verdicts are the verse's second stroke, and the commentators explain the meaning: the worst place is the Fire, and the farthest is the distance from the way, and the two together are the chapter's answer to the mockery of the previous verses, and the answer is that the mocker's end is the worst and the farthest, and the verse then closes, and the closing is the verse's last stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's transition, and the transition is the next verses' subject, and the subject is the stories, and the stories are the chapter's next teaching, and the teaching is that the dragged of this verse are the successors of the dragged of every story, and the stories will show the pattern, and the pattern is the chapter's word. The verse is the chapter's transition from the mockery to the stories, and the transition is the verdict, and the verdict is the chapter's word on the dragged, and the word is that the dragged are the successors of every mocked messenger's mockers, and the succession is the chapter's warning to its own time.",
  "35": "\"We certainly gave Moses the Book and appointed his brother Aaron as his helper\" opens the chapter's stories, and the verse is the teaching on the partnership, and the teaching is the verse's content. The verse states the two gifts: \"We certainly gave Moses the Book and appointed his brother Aaron as his helper\", and the word \"helper\" is 'waziran,' the word of the supporter, and the verse says the Book and the brother were given together, and the commentators explain the meaning: Moses asked for the brother as the supporter, and the verse records the granting, and the granting is the verse's first stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's teaching on the partnership, and the teaching is that the prophetic mission is carried by the two, and the verse then leaves the story to the next verse, and the leaving is the verse's second stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's design, and the design is the compact story, and the compact story is the chapter's method in this section, and the method is the verse's last stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's answer to the question of why the stories are told, and the answer is the next verses' subject, and the subject is the command, and the command is the chapter's next teaching, and the same request is recorded elsewhere: \"Aaron, my brother\", and the verse of the chapter keeps the partnership in the story, and the keeping is the chapter's word. The verse is the chapter's opening of the compact stories, and the opening is the partnership, and the partnership is the chapter's word on the mission, and the word is that the Book and the brother are the two gifts of the one sending, and the sending is the chapter's answer to the loner's fear.",
  "36": "\"We had ordered ˹them˺, \"Go to the people who would deny Our signs.\" Then We annihilated the deniers entirely\" continues the story, and the verse is the teaching on the command and the end, and the teaching is the verse's content. The verse states the command: \"We had ordered ˹them˺, \"Go to the people who would deny Our signs,\"\" and the verse records the sending to the deniers, and the commentators explain the meaning: the command sent the two brothers to the people whose denial was known in advance, and the sending is the verse's first stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's teaching on the mission, and the teaching is that the messenger goes to the denier with the known end, and the verse then states the end: \"Then We annihilated the deniers entirely\", and the word \"annihilated\" is 'dammarnahum,' and the verse says the deniers were destroyed entirely, and the commentators explain the meaning: the destruction of Pharaoh's people was the total, and the total is the verse's second stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's teaching on the end, and the teaching is that the denial's end is the annihilation, and the verse then closes, and the closing is the verse's last stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's answer to the deniers of its own time, and the answer is the story's pattern, and the pattern is the next verses' subject, and the subject is Noah, and Noah is the chapter's next teaching. The verse is the chapter's teaching on the known end, and the teaching is the annihilation, and the annihilation is the chapter's word on the denial, and the word is that the denier's end is the total, and the total is the chapter's answer to the partial denier who hopes for the partial escape.",
  "37": "\"And when the people of Noah rejected the messengers, We drowned them, making them an example to humanity. And We have prepared a painful punishment for the wrongdoers\" continues the stories, and the verse is the teaching on the flood, and the teaching is the verse's content. The verse states the rejection: \"And when the people of Noah rejected the messengers, We drowned them, making them an example to humanity\", and the word \"messengers\" is in the plural, and the commentators explain the meaning: the rejection of the one messenger is the rejection of all, and the verse says the drowning was the answer, and the answer is the verse's first stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's teaching on the rejection, and the teaching is that the one message is the many, and the verse then states the example: \"making them an example to humanity\", and the word \"example\" is 'ayah,' and the verse says the drowned became the sign, and the example is the verse's second stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's teaching on the warning, and the teaching is that the flood is the standing sign for the later peoples, and the verse then states the prepared: \"And We have prepared a painful punishment for the wrongdoers\", and the word \"prepared\" is 'a'tadna,' and the verse says the painful is prepared, and the prepared is the verse's last stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's teaching on the next world, and the teaching is that the drowned of this world face the prepared of the next, and the teaching is the chapter's word, and the word is the next verse's subject, and the subject is the tribes, and the tribes are the chapter's next teaching. The verse is the chapter's word on the flood, and the word is the example, and the example is the chapter's teaching on the warning, and the teaching is that the drowned became the standing sign, and the sign is the chapter's answer to the one who passes the stories without the lesson.",
  "38": "\"Also ˹We destroyed˺\"'Ād, Thamûd, and the people of the Water-pit, as well as many peoples in between continues the list, and the verse is the teaching on the generations, and the teaching is the verse's content. The verse states the three names: \"Also ˹We destroyed˺ ’Ād, Thamûd, and the people of the Water-pit, as well as many peoples in between\", and the verse names the three and the many, and the commentators explain the meaning: 'Ad was the tribe of Hud, and Thamud the tribe of Salih, and the people of the Water-pit were the people of the well who rejected their messenger, and the many are the generations between them, and the naming is the verse's first stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's teaching on the record, and the teaching is that the destroyed are many and named, and the verse then closes the list, and the closing is the verse's second stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's teaching on the pattern, and the teaching is that the names are the pattern's entries, and the verse then leaves the list, and the leaving is the verse's last stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's answer to the question of the histories, and the answer is the next verse's subject, and the subject is the lessons, and the lessons are the chapter's next teaching, and the same names are recorded elsewhere: \"Before them, the people of Noah denied ˹the truth,˺ as did the people of the Water-pit, Thamûd,\" and the verse of the chapter keeps the names, and the keeping is the chapter's word. The verse is the chapter's word on the names, and the word is the many, and the many is the chapter's teaching on the record, and the teaching is that the destroyed are the named, and the named are the chapter's answer to the one who imagines the destruction is the exception.",
  "39": "\"For each We set forth ˹various˺ lessons, and We ultimately destroyed each\" states the chapter's summary of the stories, and the verse is the teaching on the lessons, and the teaching is the verse's content. The verse states the two facts: \"For each We set forth ˹various˺ lessons, and We ultimately destroyed each\", and the word \"lessons\" is 'darabna,' the word of the striking of the parables, and the verse says every people was given the lessons, and the commentators explain the meaning: the warnings were varied for each people, and the variety is the verse's first stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's teaching on the mercy, and the teaching is that no people was destroyed without the lessons, and the verse then states the end: \"and We ultimately destroyed each\", and the word \"ultimately\" is 'tadbiran,' and the verse says the destruction was the total, and the end is the verse's second stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's teaching on the justice, and the teaching is that the lessons and the destruction are the two halves of the one record, and the verse then closes, and the closing is the verse's last stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's answer to the deniers, and the answer is that their own lessons are being given, and the giving is the chapter's word, and the word is the next verse's subject, and the subject is the ruins, and the ruins are the chapter's next teaching. The verse is the chapter's summary of the list, and the summary is the two facts, and the two facts are the chapter's word on the record, and the word is that the lessons were given and the destruction followed, and the following is the chapter's answer to the one who asks why the histories are told.",
  "40": "\"They have certainly passed by the city ˹of Sodom˺, which had been showered with a dreadful rain ˹of stones˺. Have they not seen its ruins? But they do not expect to be resurrected\" points to the visible ruins, and the verse is the teaching on the geography, and the teaching is the verse's content. The verse states the passing: \"They have certainly passed by the city ˹of Sodom˺, which had been showered with a dreadful rain ˹of stones˺\", and the word \"passed\" is 'ataw,' and the verse says the deniers of Mecca passed the ruins on their trade road, and the commentators explain the meaning: the city of Sodom was on the caravan route, and the passing is the verse's first stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's teaching on the evidence, and the teaching is that the evidence is on the road, and the verse then asks: \"Have they not seen its ruins?\" and the question is the verse's second stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's teaching on the seeing, and the teaching is that the seeing is the failure, and the verse then states the root: \"But they do not expect to be resurrected\", and the root is the verse's last stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's teaching on the cause, and the teaching is that the denial of the resurrection closes the eye to the ruins, and the teaching is the chapter's word, and the word is the next verse's subject, and the subject is the mockery, and the mockery is the chapter's next teaching. The verse is the chapter's word on the ruins, and the word is the passing, and the passing is the chapter's teaching on the evidence, and the teaching is that the evidence is on the road, and the road is the chapter's answer to the one who asks for the signs while the ruins stand.",
  "41": "\"When they see you ˹O Prophet˺, they only make fun of you, ˹saying,˺ \"Is this the one that Allah has sent as a messenger\"\" quotes the mockery, and the verse is the teaching on the ridicule, and the teaching is the verse's content. The verse states the scene: \"When they see you ˹O Prophet˺, they only make fun of you, ˹saying,˺ \"Is this the one that Allah has sent as a messenger\",\" and the word \"only\" is the verse's seal, and the verse says the seeing produces the mockery, and the commentators explain the meaning: the mockers measured the Prophet by the ordinary, and the measuring is the verse's first stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's teaching on the mockery, and the teaching is that the mockery is the deniers' whole response, and the verse then leaves the sentence to the next verse, and the leaving is the verse's second stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's design, and the design is the next verse's subject, and the subject is the claim of the devotion, and the claim is the chapter's next teaching, and the verse then closes, and the closing is the verse's last stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's answer to the mockers, and the answer is the next verse's warning, and the warning is the chapter's word. The verse is the chapter's record of the mockery, and the record is the question, and the question is the chapter's word on the mockers, and the word is that the mockers measured the messenger by the ordinary, and the measuring is the chapter's answer to every age that repeats the question, and the answer is the next verse's warning. The verse is the chapter's word on the ridicule, and the word is the only, and the only is the chapter's teaching on the mockery, and the teaching is that the mockery is the deniers' whole response, and the response is the chapter's answer to the one who expects the argument from the mocker.",
  "42": "\"He would have almost tricked us away from our gods, had we not been so devoted to them.\" ˹But˺ soon they will know, when they face the punishment, who is far astray from the ˹Right˺ Way\" completes the mockery, and the verse is the teaching on the claim and the warning, and the teaching is the verse's content. The verse completes the quote: \"He would have almost tricked us away from our gods, had we not been so devoted to them\", and the verse records the mockers' boast, and the commentators explain the meaning: the boast is the confession, and the confession is that the message almost moved them, and the almost is the verse's first stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's teaching on the boast, and the teaching is that the boast admits the message's power, and the verse then states the warning: \"˹But˺ soon they will know, when they face the punishment, who is far astray from the ˹Right˺ Way\", and the word \"soon\" is the verse's answer, and the verse says the facing of the punishment will settle the question, and the warning is the verse's second stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's teaching on the end, and the teaching is that the mockers will know who was astray, and the verse then closes, and the closing is the verse's last stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's answer to the boast, and the answer is the next verse's subject, and the subject is the desire, and the desire is the chapter's next teaching. The verse is the chapter's word on the boast, and the word is the almost, and the almost is the chapter's teaching on the confession, and the teaching is that the boast admits the message's power, and the admission is the chapter's answer to the one who claims the devotion to the idols.",
  "43": "\"Have you seen ˹O Prophet˺ the one who has taken their own desires as their god? Will you then be a keeper over them?\" states the chapter's diagnosis of the desire, and the verse is the teaching on the worshipped desire, and the teaching is the verse's content. The verse asks the question: \"Have you seen ˹O Prophet˺ the one who has taken their own desires as their god?\" and the word \"desires\" is 'hawah,' and the verse names the desire as the god, and the commentators explain the meaning: the one whose god is the desire worships the shifting, and the worship is the verse's first stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's teaching on the idolatry, and the teaching is that the desire is the most common of the gods, and the verse then asks the second question: \"Will you then be a keeper over them?\" and the question is the verse's second stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's teaching on the limit, and the teaching is that the Prophet is not the keeper of the desire-worshipper, and the verse then closes, and the closing is the verse's last stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's answer to the question of the duty, and the answer is the next verse's subject, and the subject is the cattle, and the cattle are the chapter's next teaching, and the same diagnosis is stated elsewhere: \"Have you seen ˹O Prophet˺ those who have taken their own desires as their god?\" and the verse of the chapter keeps the diagnosis, and the keeping is the chapter's word. The verse is the chapter's diagnosis of the desire, and the diagnosis is the god, and the god is the chapter's word on the idolatry, and the word is that the shifting desire is the most common of the worshipped, and the worshipped is the chapter's answer to the one who claims the freedom while serving the whim.",
  "44": "\"Or do you think that most of them listen or understand? They are only like cattle—no, more than that, they are astray from the ˹Right˺ Way\" states the chapter's verdict on the heedless, and the verse is the teaching on the listening, and the teaching is the verse's content. The verse asks the question: \"Or do you think that most of them listen or understand?\" and the verse asks about the hearing and the understanding, and the commentators explain the meaning: the question is the reproach of the Prophet's own hope, and the reproach is the verse's first stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's teaching on the audience, and the teaching is that most of the mockers are not hearers, and the verse then states the verdict: \"They are only like cattle—no, more than that, they are astray from the ˹Right˺ Way\", and the word \"cattle\" is 'an'am,' and the verse says the heedless are like the cattle, and then the verse corrects itself: \"no, more than that,\" and the commentators explain the meaning: the cattle follow the guide, and the heedless human refuses the guide, and the refusal is the deeper straying, and the correction is the verse's second stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's teaching on the responsibility, and the teaching is that the human who does not use the hearing is below the beast that has none, and the verse then closes, and the closing is the verse's last stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's answer to the mockers, and the answer is the next verses' subject, and the subject is the signs, and the signs are the chapter's next teaching. The verse is the chapter's verdict on the heedless, and the verdict is the cattle, and the cattle is the chapter's word on the responsibility, and the word is that the human who refuses the guide is below the beast that follows it, and the below is the chapter's answer to the one who boasts of the freedom.",
  "45": "\"Have you not seen how your Lord extends the shade—He could have simply made it ˹remain˺ still if He so willed—then We make the sun its guide\" opens the chapter's signs, and the verse is the teaching on the shade, and the teaching is the verse's content. The verse asks the question: \"Have you not seen how your Lord extends the shade—He could have simply made it ˹remain˺ still if He so willed—then We make the sun its guide,\" and the word \"extends\" is 'madda,' and the verse says the shade is extended, and the commentators explain the meaning: the shade of the morning is stretched over the land, and the verse says God could have made it still, and the verse then says the sun is made its guide, and the guiding is the verse's first stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's teaching on the design, and the teaching is that the shade and the sun are the two hands of the one arrangement, and the verse then leaves the sentence to the next verse, and the leaving is the verse's second stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's design, and the design is the next verse's subject, and the subject is the retreat, and the retreat is the chapter's next teaching, and the verse then closes, and the closing is the verse's last stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's answer to the question of the signs, and the answer is the shade, and the shade is the chapter's word. The verse is the chapter's first sign of the section, and the sign is the shade, and the shade is the chapter's word on the design, and the word is that the shade and the sun are the two hands of the one arrangement, and the arrangement is the chapter's answer to the one who sees the world as the chance.",
  "46": "\"causing the shade to retreat gradually\" completes the shade's picture, and the verse is the teaching on the gradual, and the teaching is the verse's content. The verse states the retreat: \"causing the shade to retreat gradually\", and the word \"gradually\" is 'qabdan yasiran,' the word of the easy pulling, and the verse says the shade is pulled back little by little, and the commentators explain the meaning: the shade does not vanish at once but withdraws with the sun's climb, and the gradual is the verse's first stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's teaching on the gentleness, and the teaching is that the world's changes are the gentle pulling, and the verse then closes, and the closing is the verse's second stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's design, and the design is the next verse's subject, and the subject is the night, and the night is the chapter's next teaching, and the verse is the chapter's shortest sign, and the shortness is the verse's last stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's teaching on the notice, and the teaching is that the shortest verse carries the shade's whole lesson, and the lesson is the chapter's word. The verse is the chapter's shortest sign, and the sign is the gradual, and the gradual is the chapter's word on the gentleness, and the word is that the world's changes are the gentle pulling, and the pulling is the chapter's answer to the one who imagines the changes are the chaos. The verse is the chapter's closing of the shade's picture, and the closing is the gentle, and the gentle is the chapter's teaching on the notice, and the teaching is that the shortest verse carries the shade's whole lesson, and the lesson is the chapter's answer to the one who misses the small signs.",
  "47": "\"He is the One Who has made the night for you as a cover, and ˹made˺ sleep for resting, and the day for rising\" continues the signs, and the verse is the teaching on the night, and the teaching is the verse's content. The verse states the three gifts: \"He is the One Who has made the night for you as a cover, and ˹made˺ sleep for resting, and the day for rising\", and the verse names the night and the sleep and the day, and the commentators explain the meaning: the night is the covering, and the sleep is the resting, and the day is the rising, and the three are the verse's first stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's teaching on the provision, and the teaching is that the day's cycle is the built shelter, and the verse then closes, and the closing is the verse's second stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's design, and the design is the next verse's subject, and the subject is the winds, and the winds are the chapter's next teaching, and the verse is the chapter's word on the night, and the word is the verse's last stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's teaching on the gratitude, and the teaching is that the night's cover and the day's rising are the gifts, and the gifts are the chapter's answer to the heedless, and the answer is the chapter's word. The verse is the chapter's word on the night, and the word is the cover, and the cover is the chapter's teaching on the provision, and the teaching is that the night and the sleep and the day are the built shelter, and the shelter is the chapter's answer to the one who sleeps and rises without the gratitude. The verse is the chapter's word on the night's gifts, and the word is the three, and the three is the chapter's teaching on the gratitude, and the teaching is that the cover and the rest and the rising are the given, and the given are the chapter's answer to the one who takes the day's cycle for granted.",
  "48": "\"And He is the One Who sends the winds ushering in His mercy, and We send down pure rain from the sky\" continues the signs, and the verse is the teaching on the winds, and the teaching is the verse's content. The verse states the two sendings: \"And He is the One Who sends the winds ushering in His mercy, and We send down pure rain from the sky,\" and the word \"ushering\" is 'nushuran,' the word of the spreading, and the verse says the winds go before the mercy, and the commentators explain the meaning: the winds are the messengers of the rain, and the rain is the mercy, and the verse names the rain \"pure,\" and the purity is the verse's first stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's teaching on the provision, and the teaching is that the wind and the rain are the two sendings of the one mercy, and the verse then closes, and the closing is the verse's second stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's design, and the design is the next verse's subject, and the subject is the revival, and the revival is the chapter's next teaching, and the verse is the chapter's word on the wind, and the word is the verse's last stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's teaching on the chain, and the teaching is that the wind carries the mercy to the land, and the carrying is the chapter's word. The verse is the chapter's word on the wind, and the word is the ushering, and the ushering is the chapter's teaching on the chain, and the teaching is that the wind carries the mercy to the land, and the carrying is the chapter's answer to the one who sees the rain without the sender. The verse is the chapter's word on the wind's mission, and the word is the pure, and the pure is the chapter's teaching on the provision, and the teaching is that the wind and the rain are the two sendings of the one mercy, and the mercy is the chapter's answer to the one who reads the weather without the sender.",
  "49": "\"giving life to a lifeless land, and providing water for countless animals and humans of Our Own creation\" completes the rain's chain, and the verse is the teaching on the revival, and the teaching is the verse's content. The verse states the two results: \"giving life to a lifeless land, and providing water for countless animals and humans of Our Own creation\", and the word \"lifeless\" is 'maytah,' and the verse says the dead land is revived, and the verse then names the drinkers: the animals and the humans, and the commentators explain the meaning: the revival of the land is the standing proof of the revival of the dead, and the naming of the drinkers ties the water to the life, and the tying is the verse's first stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's teaching on the resurrection, and the teaching is that the rain's revival is the resurrection's sample, and the verse then closes, and the closing is the verse's second stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's design, and the design is the next verse's subject, and the subject is the dispersion, and the dispersion is the chapter's next teaching, and the verse is the chapter's word on the revival, and the word is the verse's last stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's teaching on the sign, and the teaching is that the dead land and the dead bones are one matter to the One who revives both, and the matter is the chapter's word. The verse is the chapter's word on the revival, and the word is the lifeless, and the lifeless is the chapter's teaching on the resurrection, and the teaching is that the dead land and the dead bones are one matter, and the matter is the chapter's answer to the denier of the rising.",
  "50": "\"We certainly disperse it among them so they may be mindful, but most people persist in ungratefulness\" states the purpose of the dispersion, and the verse is the teaching on the mindfulness, and the teaching is the verse's content. The verse states the purpose: \"We certainly disperse it among them so they may be mindful,\" and the word \"disperse\" is 'sarrafnahu,' and the verse says the rain is dispersed among the people, and the commentators explain the meaning: the dispersion of the provision among the lands is the opportunity of the mindfulness, and the opportunity is the verse's first stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's teaching on the purpose, and the teaching is that the signs are spread for the remembering, and the verse then states the response: \"but most people persist in ungratefulness\", and the word \"ungratefulness\" is 'kufuran,' and the verse says the most persist in the denial, and the response is the verse's second stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's teaching on the failure, and the teaching is that the dispersed signs are met with the persistent denial, and the verse then closes, and the closing is the verse's last stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's answer to the question of the signs, and the answer is the next verse's subject, and the subject is the warner, and the warner is the chapter's next teaching. The verse is the chapter's word on the dispersion, and the word is the purpose, and the purpose is the chapter's teaching on the mindfulness, and the teaching is that the signs are spread for the remembering, and the remembering is the chapter's answer to the persistent denial. The verse is the chapter's word on the response, and the word is the most, and the most is the chapter's teaching on the failure, and the teaching is that the dispersed signs are met with the persistent denial, and the denial is the chapter's answer to the question of why the signs do not turn the hearts.",
  "51": "\"Had We willed, We could have easily sent a warner to every society\" states the chapter's word on the sending, and the verse is the teaching on the mercy, and the teaching is the verse's content. The verse states the possibility: \"Had We willed, We could have easily sent a warner to every society\", and the word \"easily\" is 'ba'athna,' and the verse says the sending to every society was within the will, and the commentators explain the meaning: God did not scatter the warners to every town but gathered the final warning in the one messenger, and the gathering is the verse's first stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's teaching on the arrangement, and the teaching is that the single warner to the worlds is the greater mercy, and the verse then closes, and the closing is the verse's second stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's design, and the design is the next verse's subject, and the subject is the striving, and the striving is the chapter's next teaching, and the verse is the chapter's word on the sending, and the word is the verse's last stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's teaching on the responsibility, and the teaching is that the single warning carries the whole duty, and the duty is the chapter's word. The verse is the chapter's word on the sending, and the word is the will, and the will is the chapter's teaching on the mercy, and the teaching is that the single warner to the worlds is the gathered mercy, and the gathering is the chapter's answer to the one who asks for the scatter of the warners. The verse is the chapter's word on the responsibility, and the word is the single, and the single is the chapter's teaching on the duty, and the teaching is that the one warning carries the whole, and the whole is the chapter's answer to the one who waits for another warner.",
  "52": "\"So do not yield to the disbelievers, but strive diligently against them with this ˹Quran˺\" states the chapter's command to the Prophet, and the verse is the teaching on the striving, and the teaching is the verse's content. The verse states the two commands: \"So do not yield to the disbelievers, but strive diligently against them with this ˹Quran˺\", and the word \"yield\" is 'tuti',' and the word \"strive\" is 'jahidhum,' and the verse says the striving is with the Quran, and the commentators explain the meaning: the striving against the disbelievers is the striving of the truth, and the Quran is the weapon, and the saying is the verse's first stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's teaching on the struggle, and the teaching is that the greatest striving is the delivering of the Book against the denial, and the same command is stated elsewhere: \"O Prophet! Struggle against the disbelievers and the hypocrites, and be firm with them\", and the verse of the chapter keeps the striving in the section of the signs, and the keeping is the chapter's design, and the verse then closes, and the closing is the verse's second stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's design, and the design is the next verse's subject, and the subject is the two seas, and the seas are the chapter's next teaching, and the verse is the chapter's word on the striving, and the word is the verse's last stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's teaching on the caller, and the teaching is that the caller does not yield and does not rest, and the calling is the chapter's word. The verse is the chapter's word on the striving, and the word is the Quran, and the Quran is the chapter's teaching on the struggle, and the teaching is that the greatest striving is the delivering of the Book, and the delivering is the chapter's answer to the one who thinks the struggle is only the sword.",
  "53": "\"And He is the One Who merges the two bodies of water: one fresh and palatable and the other salty and bitter, placing between them a barrier they cannot cross\" closes the section of the signs, and the verse is the teaching on the two seas, and the teaching is the verse's content. The verse states the sign: \"And He is the One Who merges the two bodies of water: one fresh and palatable and the other salty and bitter, placing between them a barrier they cannot cross\", and the word \"merges\" is 'maraja,' and the verse says the two waters meet, and the commentators explain the meaning: the fresh river meets the salty sea, and the barrier holds each in its nature, and the barrier is the verse's first stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's teaching on the power, and the teaching is that the one water is kept in its two natures, and the same sign is stated elsewhere: \"He merges the two bodies of ˹fresh and salt˺ water\", and \"yet between them is a barrier they never cross\". and the verse of the chapter keeps the sign, and the keeping is the chapter's design, and the verse then closes, and the closing is the verse's last stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's answer to the question of the signs, and the answer is the two seas, and the two seas are the chapter's word, and the word is the next verses' subject, and the subject is the human creation, and the creation is the chapter's next teaching. The verse is the chapter's closing of the signs, and the closing is the barrier, and the barrier is the chapter's teaching on the power, and the teaching is that the one water is kept in its two natures, and the keeping is the chapter's answer to the one who sees the meeting without the keeper, and the answer is the two seas, and the two seas are the chapter's word, and the word is the next verses' subject, and the subject is the human creation, and the creation is the chapter's next teaching.",
  "54": "\"And He is the One Who creates human beings from a ˹humble˺ liquid, then establishes for them bonds of kinship and marriage. For your Lord is Most Capable\" closes the chapter's signs with the human being's own creation, and the verse is the teaching on the origin and the bond, and the teaching is the verse's content. The verse states the two acts: \"And He is the One Who creates human beings from a ˹humble˺ liquid, then establishes for them bonds of kinship and marriage\", and the word \"humble\" is 'mahin,' the word of the lowly, and the verse says the human being is made from the lowly drop, and the verse then says the bonds of the kinship and the marriage are established, and the commentators explain the meaning: the lowly origin and the high bond are the two ends of the one design, and the design is the verse's first stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's teaching on the origin, and the teaching is that the pride of the human being is cured by his origin, and the verse then states the power: \"For your Lord is Most Capable\", and the word \"Capable\" is 'qadir,' and the verse says the power covers the drop and the bond, and the saying is the verse's second stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's teaching on the ability, and the teaching is that the One who made the bonds from the drop can remake the bones, and the verse then closes, and the closing is the verse's last stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's answer to the denier of the resurrection, and the answer is the origin, and the origin is the chapter's word, and the word is the next verse's subject, and the subject is the idols, and the idols are the chapter's next teaching.",
  "55": "\"Yet they worship besides Allah what can neither benefit nor harm them. And the disbeliever always collaborates against their Lord\" states the chapter's verdict on the idolatry, and the verse is the teaching on the colaborer, and the teaching is the verse's content. The verse states the worship: \"Yet they worship besides Allah what can neither benefit nor harm them\", and the word \"yet\" is the verse's contrast, and the verse says the worshipped are the powerless, and the commentators explain the meaning: the verse returns to the chapter's standing argument against the idols, and the returning is the verse's first stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's teaching on the absurdity, and the teaching is that the worshipped without the benefit and the harm is the nothing, and the verse then states the deeper fact: \"And the disbeliever always collaborates against their Lord\", and the word \"collaborates\" is 'zahiran,' the word of the backing, and the verse says the disbeliever is the helper of the false against the true, and the commentators explain the meaning: the disbeliever's worship is not the neutral error but the standing against, and the saying is the verse's second stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's teaching on the position, and the teaching is that the idolater has chosen the side, and the verse then closes, and the closing is the verse's last stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's answer to the idolatry, and the answer is the next verse's subject, and the subject is the mission, and the mission is the chapter's next teaching. The verse is the chapter's closing of the idolatry's case, and the closing is the colaborer, and the colaborer is the chapter's word on the position, and the word is that the idolater's worship is the standing against the true, and the standing is the chapter's answer to the one who claims the neutrality, and the answer is the next verse's subject, and the subject is the mission, and the mission is the chapter's next teaching.",
  "56": "\"And We have sent you ˹O Prophet˺ only as a deliverer of good news and a warner\" states the mission, and the verse is the teaching on the two faces, and the teaching is the verse's content. The verse states the mission: \"And We have sent you ˹O Prophet˺ only as a deliverer of good news and a warner\", and the word \"only\" is the verse's boundary, and the verse names the two faces of the sending, and the commentators explain the meaning: the good news is for the believer, and the warning is for the denier, and the two faces are the verse's first stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's teaching on the mission, and the teaching is that the messenger's work is the delivering, and the verse then closes, and the closing is the verse's second stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's design, and the design is the next verse's subject, and the subject is the reward, and the reward is the chapter's next teaching, and the verse is the chapter's word on the boundary, and the word is the verse's last stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's teaching on the duty, and the teaching is that the deliverer is not the keeper, and the keeping is the Lord's, and the teaching is the chapter's word. The verse is the chapter's word on the boundary, and the boundary is the only, and the only is the chapter's teaching on the duty, and the teaching is that the deliverer is not the keeper, and the keeping is the Lord's, and the teaching is the chapter's answer to the question of the messenger's power, and the answer is the two faces, and the two faces are the chapter's word, and the word is the next verse's subject, and the subject is the reward, and the reward is the chapter's next teaching.",
  "57": "\"Say, \"I do not ask you for any reward for this ˹message˺, but whoever wishes, let them pursue the Way to their Lord\"\" states the Prophet's detachment, and the verse is the teaching on the free message, and the teaching is the verse's content. The verse quotes the declaration: \"Say, \"I do not ask you for any reward for this ˹message˺, but whoever wishes, let them pursue the Way to their Lord\",\" and the word \"reward\" is 'ajr,' and the verse says the message is free, and the commentators explain the meaning: the free message is the proof of the sincerity, and the sincerity is the verse's first stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's teaching on the messenger, and the teaching is that the messenger asks nothing for himself, and the verse then states the invitation: \"but whoever wishes, let them pursue the Way to their Lord\", and the word \"wishes\" is 'sha',' and the verse says the way is open to the willing, and the invitation is the verse's second stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's teaching on the choice, and the teaching is that the way to the Lord is the willing one's own road, and the verse then closes, and the closing is the verse's last stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's answer to the accusation of the profit, and the answer is the free message, and the free message is the chapter's word, and the word is the next verse's subject, and the subject is the trust, and the trust is the chapter's next teaching. The verse is the chapter's word on the free message, and the word is the sincerity, and the sincerity is the chapter's teaching on the messenger, and the teaching is that the messenger asks nothing for himself, and the asking of nothing is the chapter's answer to the accusation of the profit, and the answer is the invitation, and the invitation is the chapter's word, and the word is the next verse's subject, and the subject is the trust, and the trust is the chapter's next teaching.",
  "58": "\"Put your trust in the Ever-Living, Who never dies, and glorify His praises. Sufficient is He as All-Aware of the sins of His servants\" commands the trust, and the verse is the teaching on the reliance, and the teaching is the verse's content. The verse states the two commands: \"Put your trust in the Ever-Living, Who never dies, and glorify His praises\", and the word \"Ever-Living\" is 'al-Hayy,' and the verse says the trust is placed in the One who never dies, and the commentators explain the meaning: the trust in the dying is the loss, and the trust in the Living is the gain, and the saying is the verse's first stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's teaching on the reliance, and the teaching is that the believer's reliance is on the deathless, and the verse then states the sufficiency: \"Sufficient is He as All-Aware of the sins of His servants\", and the word \"Sufficient\" is 'kafa,' and the verse says the All-Aware is the enough, and the commentators explain the meaning: the One who knows the sins is the One who forgives them, and the knowing is the verse's second stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's teaching on the comfort, and the teaching is that the sins are known and the door is open, and the verse then closes, and the closing is the verse's last stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's answer to the fear, and the answer is the Living and the Aware, and the two names are the chapter's word, and the word is the next verse's subject, and the subject is the creation, and the creation is the chapter's next teaching. The verse is the chapter's word on the reliance, and the word is the deathless, and the deathless is the chapter's teaching on the trust, and the teaching is that the trust in the dying is the loss, and the trust in the Living is the gain, and the gain is the chapter's answer to the fear, and the answer is the two names, and the two names are the chapter's word, and the word is the next verse's subject, and the subject is the creation, and the creation is the chapter's next teaching.",
  "59": "\"˹He is˺ the One Who created the heavens and the earth and everything in between in six Days, then established Himself on the Throne. ˹He is˺ the Most Compassionate! Ask ˹none other than˺ the All-Knowledgeable about Himself\" states the chapter's word on the creation, and the verse is the teaching on the Throne, and the teaching is the verse's content. The verse states the creation: \"˹He is˺ the One Who created the heavens and the earth and everything in between in six Days, then established Himself on the Throne\", and the word \"established\" is 'istawa,' and the verse says the establishment on the Throne followed the six Days, and the commentators explain the meaning: the establishment is the sovereignty's rising over the creation, and the explanation is the verse's first stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's teaching on the kingship, and the teaching is that the Creator is the seated King, and the verse then states the name: \"˹He is˺ the Most Compassionate!\" and the verse crowns the description with the mercy, and the commentators explain the meaning: the King of the Throne is the Merciful, and the crowning is the verse's second stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's teaching on the mercy, and the teaching is that the power and the mercy are the two faces of the one Lord, and the verse then commands: \"Ask ˹none other than˺ the All-Knowledgeable about Himself\", and the command is the verse's last stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's teaching on the knowledge, and the teaching is that the knowledge of God is sought from the One who knows, and the same creation is described elsewhere: \"Indeed your Lord is Allah Who created the heavens and the earth in six Days, then established Himself on the Throne\", and the verse of the chapter keeps the Throne, and the keeping is the chapter's word.",
  "60": "\"When it is said to them, \"Prostrate to the Most Compassionate,\" they ask ˹in disgust˺, \"What is 'the Most Compassionate'? Will we prostrate to whatever you order us to?\" And it only drives them farther away\" records the disbelievers' refusal, and the verse is the teaching on the name, and the teaching is the verse's content. The verse quotes the scene: \"When it is said to them, \"Prostrate to the Most Compassionate,\" they ask ˹in disgust˺, \"What is 'the Most Compassionate'? Will we prostrate to whatever you order us to?\"\" and the verse records the refusal, and the commentators explain the background: the disbelievers of Mecca knew the name Allah but claimed not to know the Most Compassionate, and the claim was the refusal, and the verse then states the result: \"And it only drives them farther away\", and the word \"farther\" is 'nufuran,' and the verse says the command to the prostration increased their distance, and the commentators explain the meaning: the truth that is refused with the mockery pushes the refuser farther, and the saying is the verse's first stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's teaching on the refusal, and the teaching is that the mockery of the name is the mockery of the mercy, and the verse then closes, and the closing is the verse's second stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's design, and the design is the next verse's subject, and the subject is the constellations, and the constellations are the chapter's next teaching, and the verse is the chapter's word on the name, and the word is the verse's last stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's teaching on the answer, and the teaching is that the name they refused is the name of the signs, and the signs are the chapter's word.",
  "61": "\"Blessed is the One Who has placed constellations in the sky, as well as a ˹radiant˺ lamp and a luminous moon\" turns to the signs of the sky, and the verse is the teaching on the constellations, and the teaching is the verse's content. The verse states the blessing: \"Blessed is the One Who has placed constellations in the sky, as well as a ˹radiant˺ lamp and a luminous moon\", and the word \"constellations\" is 'buruj,' and the verse names the three lights, and the commentators explain the meaning: the constellations are the fixed stations, and the lamp is the sun, and the luminous moon is the moon, and the three are the verse's first stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's teaching on the sky, and the teaching is that the sky's lights are the placed, and the verse then closes, and the closing is the verse's second stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's design, and the design is the next verse's subject, and the subject is the alternation, and the alternation is the chapter's next teaching, and the verse is the chapter's word on the sky, and the word is the verse's last stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's teaching on the guidance, and the teaching is that the placed lights guide the traveler, and the guiding is the chapter's word. The verse is the chapter's word on the sky, and the word is the placed, and the placed is the chapter's teaching on the guidance, and the teaching is that the sky's lights guide the traveler, and the guiding is the chapter's answer to the mockers of the name, and the answer is the blessing, and the blessing is the chapter's word, and the word is the next verse's subject, and the subject is the alternation, and the alternation is the chapter's next teaching.",
  "62": "\"And He is the One Who causes the day and the night to alternate, ˹as a sign˺ for whoever desires to be mindful or to be grateful\" continues the signs, and the verse is the teaching on the alternation, and the teaching is the verse's content. The verse states the alternation: \"And He is the One Who causes the day and the night to alternate, ˹as a sign˺ for whoever desires to be mindful or to be grateful\", and the word \"alternate\" is 'khalafahum,' and the verse says the day and the night follow one another, and the commentators explain the meaning: the following is the sign, and the sign is for the two kinds of the responders, and the two kinds are the verse's first stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's teaching on the sign, and the teaching is that the alternation serves the mindful and the grateful, and the verse then closes, and the closing is the verse's second stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's design, and the design is the next verse's subject, and the subject is the servants, and the servants are the chapter's next teaching, and the verse is the chapter's word on the two responders, and the word is the verse's last stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's teaching on the choice, and the teaching is that the mindful remembers and the grateful thanks, and the two are the chapter's word. The verse is the chapter's word on the alternation, and the word is the sign, and the sign is the chapter's teaching on the two responders, and the teaching is that the mindful remembers and the grateful thanks, and the two are the chapter's answer to the heedless, and the answer is the next verse's subject, and the subject is the servants, and the servants are the chapter's next teaching, and the next teaching is the great list.",
  "63": "\"The ˹true˺ servants of the Most Compassionate are those who walk on the earth humbly, and when the foolish address them ˹improperly˺, they only respond with peace\" opens the chapter's great description of the servants, and the verse is the teaching on the walk, and the teaching is the verse's content. The verse states the title: \"The ˹true˺ servants of the Most Compassionate are those who walk on the earth humbly,\" and the word \"servants\" is 'ibad,' and the verse names the servants by the Merciful, and the commentators note the honour: the servants are named by the mercy rather than the fear, and the noting is the verse's first stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's teaching on the title, and the teaching is that the servant of the Merciful is the honoured, and the verse then states the walk: \"and when the foolish address them ˹improperly˺, they only respond with peace\", and the word \"peace\" is 'salama,' and the verse says the response to the foolish is the peace, and the commentators explain the meaning: the peace is the word of the parting, and the servant does not trade the insult for the insult, and the saying is the verse's second stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's teaching on the manner, and the teaching is that the humble walk and the peaceful answer are the servant's two marks, and the verse then closes, and the closing is the verse's last stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's opening of the list, and the list is the next verses' subject, and the subject is the night, and the night is the chapter's next teaching. The verse is the chapter's opening of the great list, and the opening is the title, and the title is the chapter's teaching on the honour, and the teaching is that the servants are named by the Merciful rather than the fear, and the naming is the chapter's answer to the question of who the true servants are, and the answer is the list, and the list is the next verses' subject, and the subject is the night, and the night is the chapter's next teaching.",
  "64": "\"˹They are˺ those who spend ˹a good portion of˺ the night, prostrating themselves and standing before their Lord\" continues the list, and the verse is the teaching on the night, and the teaching is the verse's content. The verse states the mark: \"˹They are˺ those who spend ˹a good portion of˺ the night, prostrating themselves and standing before their Lord\", and the word \"spend\" is 'yabitun,' and the verse says the servants pass the night in the prostration and the standing, and the commentators explain the meaning: the night of the servant is the prayer, and the prayer is the verse's first stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's teaching on the night, and the teaching is that the servant's night is the worship while the world sleeps, and the verse then closes, and the closing is the verse's second stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's design, and the design is the next verse's subject, and the subject is the prayer, and the prayer is the chapter's next teaching, and the verse is the chapter's word on the secret worship, and the word is the verse's last stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's teaching on the sincerity, and the teaching is that the night prayer is the worship that no one sees, and the unseen worship is the chapter's word. The verse is the chapter's word on the night, and the word is the secret, and the secret is the chapter's teaching on the sincerity, and the teaching is that the night prayer is the worship that no one sees, and the unseen worship is the chapter's answer to the show, and the answer is the prostration and the standing, and the two are the chapter's word, and the word is the next verse's subject, and the subject is the prayer, and the prayer is the chapter's next teaching.",
  "65": "\"˹They are˺ those who pray, \"Our Lord! Keep the punishment of Hell away from us, for its punishment is indeed unrelenting\"\" continues the list, and the verse is the teaching on the prayer, and the teaching is the verse's content. The verse states the prayer: \"˹They are˺ those who pray, \"Our Lord! Keep the punishment of Hell away from us, for its punishment is indeed unrelenting\",\" and the word \"unrelenting\" is 'gharama,' and the verse says the punishment is the clinging debt, and the commentators explain the meaning: the servant fears the punishment even in the worship, and the fearing is the verse's first stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's teaching on the fear, and the teaching is that the servant's prayer carries the fear and the hope together, and the verse then leaves the sentence to the next verse, and the leaving is the verse's second stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's design, and the design is the next verse's subject, and the subject is the place, and the place is the chapter's next teaching, and the verse is the chapter's word on the prayer, and the word is the verse's last stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's teaching on the balance, and the teaching is that the one who worships still asks for the keeping, and the asking is the chapter's word. The verse is the chapter's word on the prayer, and the word is the fear, and the fear is the chapter's teaching on the balance, and the teaching is that the servant's prayer carries the fear and the hope together, and the carrying is the chapter's answer to the one who prays without the heart, and the answer is the next verse's subject, and the subject is the place, and the place is the chapter's next teaching.",
  "66": "\"It is certainly an evil place to settle and reside\" completes the prayer, and the verse is the teaching on the place, and the teaching is the verse's content. The verse completes the prayer: \"It is certainly an evil place to settle and reside\", and the word \"settle\" is 'mustaqarran,' and the word \"reside\" is 'muqama,' and the verse says the Hell is the evil settlement and the evil residence, and the commentators explain the meaning: the two words cover the temporary and the permanent, and the covering is the verse's first stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's teaching on the place, and the teaching is that the Hell is the evil in both the staying and the resting, and the verse then closes, and the closing is the verse's second stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's design, and the design is the next verse's subject, and the subject is the spending, and the spending is the chapter's next teaching, and the verse is the chapter's word on the fear, and the word is the verse's last stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's teaching on the motive, and the teaching is that the servant's fear of the evil place feeds his worship, and the feeding is the chapter's word. The verse is the chapter's word on the place, and the word is the evil, and the evil is the chapter's teaching on the motive, and the teaching is that the servant's fear of the evil place feeds his worship, and the feeding is the chapter's answer to the heedless prayer, and the answer is the next verse's subject, and the subject is the spending, and the spending is the chapter's next teaching. The verse is the chapter's closing of the prayer, and the closing is the two words, and the two words are the chapter's teaching on the permanence, and the teaching is that the Hell's evil is the permanent, and the permanent is the chapter's answer to the one who hopes the punishment ends, and the answer is the next verse's subject, and the subject is the spending, and the spending is the chapter's next teaching.",
  "67": "\"˹They are˺ those who spend neither wastefully nor stingily, but moderately in between\" continues the list, and the verse is the teaching on the spending, and the teaching is the verse's content. The verse states the mark: \"˹They are˺ those who spend neither wastefully nor stingily, but moderately in between\", and the word \"wastefully\" is 'israf,' and the word \"stingily\" is 'iqtar,' and the verse says the servant's spending is the middle, and the commentators explain the meaning: the middle of the spending is the believer's path, and the path is the verse's first stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's teaching on the balance, and the teaching is that the wealth of the servant is the measured, and the same teaching is stated elsewhere: \"Do not be so tight-fisted, for you will be blameworthy; nor so open-handed, for you will end up in poverty\", and the verse of the chapter keeps the middle, and the keeping is the chapter's design, and the verse then closes, and the closing is the verse's second stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's design, and the design is the next verse's subject, and the subject is the sins, and the sins are the chapter's next teaching, and the verse is the chapter's word on the wealth, and the word is the verse's last stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's teaching on the moderation, and the teaching is that the waste and the stinginess are the two edges, and the middle is the chapter's word. The verse is the chapter's word on the wealth, and the word is the middle, and the middle is the chapter's teaching on the moderation, and the teaching is that the waste and the stinginess are the two edges, and the edges are the chapter's answer to the two diseases of the wealth, and the answer is the measured, and the measured is the chapter's word, and the word is the next verse's subject, and the subject is the three sins, and the sins are the chapter's next teaching.",
  "68": "\"˹They are˺ those who do not invoke any other god besides Allah, nor take a ˹human˺ life—made sacred by Allah—except with ˹legal˺ right, nor commit fornication. And whoever does ˹any of˺ this will face the penalty\" continues the list, and the verse is the teaching on the three great sins, and the teaching is the verse's content. The verse states the three: \"˹They are˺ those who do not invoke any other god besides Allah, nor take a ˹human˺ life—made sacred by Allah—except with ˹legal˺ right, nor commit fornication,\" and the verse names the three, and the commentators explain the meaning: the three are the great sins, and the verse guards them in the negative, and the guarding is the verse's first stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's teaching on the purity, and the teaching is that the servant's worship is the one God's, and the servant's hand holds the life sacred, and the servant's desire stays within the lawful, and the verse then states the warning: \"And whoever does ˹any of˺ this will face the penalty\", and the word \"penalty\" is 'athaman,' and the verse says the doer faces the sin's wage, and the warning is the verse's second stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's teaching on the weight, and the teaching is that the three sins carry the wage, and the verse then closes, and the closing is the verse's last stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's design, and the design is the next verse's subject, and the subject is the multiplication, and the multiplication is the chapter's next teaching, and the verse is the chapter's word on the three, and the word is the chapter's teaching on the boundaries, and the teaching is that the servant of the Merciful is the keeper of the three boundaries, and the keeping is the chapter's word.",
  "69": "\"Their punishment will be multiplied on the Day of Judgment, and they will remain in it forever, in disgrace\" states the wage of the three sins, and the verse is the teaching on the multiplication, and the teaching is the verse's content. The verse states the wage: \"Their punishment will be multiplied on the Day of Judgment, and they will remain in it forever, in disgrace\", and the word \"multiplied\" is 'yuda'af,' and the verse says the punishment is doubled, and the commentators explain the meaning: the one who persists in the great sins faces the multiplied, and the multiplying is the verse's first stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's teaching on the weight, and the teaching is that the persistence is the doubling, and the verse then states the two qualities of the punishment: \"and they will remain in it forever, in disgrace\", and the word \"disgrace\" is 'muhanan,' and the verse says the staying is with the disgrace, and the two qualities are the verse's second stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's teaching on the end, and the teaching is that the multiplied punishment is the permanent and the disgraceful, and the verse then closes, and the closing is the verse's last stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's design, and the design is the next verse's subject, and the subject is the repentance, and the repentance is the chapter's next teaching, and the verse is the chapter's word on the wage, and the word is the chapter's warning, and the warning is the chapter's word. The verse is the chapter's word on the wage, and the word is the multiplied, and the multiplied is the chapter's teaching on the weight, and the teaching is that the persistence is the doubling, and the doubling is the chapter's answer to the one who takes the great sin lightly, and the answer is the disgrace, and the disgrace is the chapter's word, and the word is the next verse's subject, and the subject is the door of the repentance, and the door is the chapter's next teaching.",
  "70": "\"As for those who repent, believe, and do good deeds, they are the ones whose evil deeds Allah will change into good deeds. For Allah is All-Forgiving, Most Merciful\" opens the door after the wage, and the verse is the teaching on the change, and the teaching is the verse's content. The verse states the three conditions: \"As for those who repent, believe, and do good deeds, they are the ones whose evil deeds Allah will change into good deeds\", and the word \"change\" is 'yubaddilu,' and the verse says the evil deeds are changed into the good, and the commentators explain the meaning: the change is the mercy's greatest gift, and the gift is the verse's first stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's teaching on the change, and the teaching is that the repentance does not merely erase but replaces, and the verse then states the ground: \"For Allah is All-Forgiving, Most Merciful\", and the two names are the verse's second stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's teaching on the source, and the teaching is that the change is the work of the two names, and the verse then closes, and the closing is the verse's last stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's answer to the wage of the previous verse, and the answer is the door, and the door is the chapter's word, and the same door is announced elsewhere: \"Say, ˹O Prophet, that Allah says,˺ \"O My servants who have exceeded the limits against their own souls! Do not lose hope in Allah's mercy,\"\" and the verse of the chapter keeps the door, and the keeping is the chapter's design, and the design is the next verse's subject, and the subject is the turning, and the turning is the chapter's next teaching. The verse is the chapter's teaching on the replacement, and the teaching is the mercy, and the mercy is the chapter's word on the change, and the word is that the repentance does not merely erase but replaces, and the replacing is the chapter's answer to the despair, and the answer is the two names, and the two names are the chapter's word.",
  "71": "\"And whoever repents and does good has truly turned to Allah properly\" states the seal of the turning, and the verse is the teaching on the proper, and the teaching is the verse's content. The verse states the seal: \"And whoever repents and does good has truly turned to Allah properly\", and the word \"properly\" is 'mataban,' and the verse says the turning of the repentant is the proper, and the commentators explain the meaning: the proper turning is the repentance joined to the deed, and the joining is the verse's first stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's teaching on the turning, and the teaching is that the word of the repentance is completed by the deed, and the verse then closes, and the closing is the verse's second stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's design, and the design is the next verse's subject, and the subject is the false witness, and the false witness is the chapter's next teaching, and the verse is the chapter's word on the return, and the word is the verse's last stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's teaching on the acceptance, and the teaching is that the proper turning is the accepted, and the accepted is the chapter's word. The verse is the chapter's word on the return, and the word is the proper, and the proper is the chapter's teaching on the acceptance, and the teaching is that the word of the repentance is completed by the deed, and the completion is the chapter's answer to the one who repents with the tongue alone, and the answer is the next verse's subject, and the subject is the witness, and the witness is the chapter's next teaching. The verse is the chapter's teaching on the completion, and the teaching is the deed, and the deed is the chapter's word on the turning, and the word is that the word of the repentance is completed by the deed, and the completing is the chapter's answer to the tongue alone, and the answer is the next verse's subject, and the subject is the witness, and the witness is the chapter's next teaching.",
  "72": "\"˹They are˺ those who do not bear false witness, and when they come across falsehood, they pass ˹it˺ by with dignity\" continues the list, and the verse is the teaching on the witness, and the teaching is the verse's content. The verse states the two marks: \"˹They are˺ those who do not bear false witness, and when they come across falsehood, they pass ˹it˺ by with dignity\", and the word \"false witness\" is 'zur,' and the word \"dignity\" is 'kiraman,' and the verse says the servant does not testify the false, and the verse says the servant passes the falsehood with the dignity, and the commentators explain the meaning: the two marks are the tongue's purity and the manner's honour, and the two are the verse's first stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's teaching on the witness, and the teaching is that the servant's word is the true, and the servant's passing is the dignified, and the same manner is described elsewhere: \"When they hear slanderous talk, they turn away from it, saying, \"We are accountable for our deeds and you for yours,\"\" and the verse of the chapter keeps the two marks, and the keeping is the chapter's design, and the verse then closes, and the closing is the verse's second stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's design, and the design is the next verse's subject, and the subject is the revelation, and the revelation is the chapter's next teaching, and the verse is the chapter's word on the tongue and the manner, and the word is the verse's last stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's teaching on the dignity, and the teaching is that the servant's dignity is the passing, and the passing is the chapter's word. The verse is the chapter's word on the tongue and the manner, and the word is the dignity, and the dignity is the chapter's teaching on the passing, and the teaching is that the servant's dignity is the passing by the falsehood, and the passing is the chapter's answer to the one who stops to fight the false, and the answer is the next verse's subject, and the subject is the revelation, and the revelation is the chapter's next teaching.",
  "73": "\"˹They are˺ those who, when reminded of the revelation of their Lord, do not turn a blind eye or a deaf ear to it\" continues the list, and the verse is the teaching on the hearing, and the teaching is the verse's content. The verse states the mark: \"˹They are˺ those who, when reminded of the revelation of their Lord, do not turn a blind eye or a deaf ear to it\", and the word \"blind\" is 'khirran summan,' and the verse says the servant does not fall on the revelation as the blind and the deaf, and the commentators explain the meaning: the servant's hearing of the revelation is the awake hearing, and the hearing is the verse's first stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's teaching on the response, and the teaching is that the servant's heart is the open door to the revelation, and the verse then closes, and the closing is the verse's second stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's design, and the design is the next verses' subject, and the subject is the prayer, and the prayer is the chapter's next teaching, and the verse is the chapter's word on the hearing, and the word is the verse's last stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's teaching on the contrast, and the teaching is that the servant is the opposite of the cattle of the earlier verse, and the opposite is the chapter's word, and the list continues to its end, and the end is the chapter's next teaching. The verse is the chapter's word on the hearing, and the word is the awake, and the awake is the chapter's teaching on the contrast, and the teaching is that the servant is the opposite of the cattle of the earlier verse, and the opposite is the chapter's answer to the one who hears the revelation with the closed heart, and the answer is the list's continuation, and the continuation is the chapter's next verses' teaching.",
  "74": "\"˹They are˺ those who pray, \"Our Lord! Bless us with ˹pious˺ spouses and offspring who will be the joy of our hearts, and make us models for the righteous\"\" continues the list of the servants, and the verse is the teaching on the prayer for the family, and the teaching is the verse's content. The verse states the prayer: \"˹They are˺ those who pray, \"Our Lord! Bless us with ˹pious˺ spouses and offspring who will be the joy of our hearts, and make us models for the righteous,\"\" and the word \"joy\" is 'qurrat a'yun,' the word of the coolness of the eyes, and the verse says the servants pray for the spouses and the offspring who cool the eyes, and the commentators explain the meaning: the coolness of the eyes is the joy of the heart, and the prayer asks for the family that is the joy, and the asking is the verse's first stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's teaching on the family, and the teaching is that the servant's prayer reaches his household, and the verse then states the second asking: \"and make us models for the righteous\", and the word \"models\" is 'imaman,' the word of the leaders, and the verse says the servants ask to be the models of the God-fearing, and the commentators explain the meaning: the servant asks not for the rank over the people but for the rank of the example, and the asking is the verse's second stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's teaching on the ambition, and the teaching is that the believer's ambition is the example, and the verse then closes, and the closing is the verse's last stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's design, and the design is the next verse's subject, and the subject is the reward, and the reward is the chapter's next teaching, and the verse is the chapter's word on the family, and the word is the chapter's teaching on the praying heart, and the teaching is that the servant's prayer covers the home and the community, and the covering is the chapter's word.",
  "75": "\"It is they who will be rewarded with ˹elevated˺ mansions ˹in Paradise˺ for their perseverance, and will be received with salutations and ˹greetings of˺ peace\" states the reward of the servants, and the verse is the teaching on the mansions, and the teaching is the verse's content. The verse states the reward: \"It is they who will be rewarded with ˹elevated˺ mansions ˹in Paradise˺ for their perseverance, and will be received with salutations and ˹greetings of˺ peace\", and the word \"mansions\" is 'ghurfah,' the word of the upper rooms, and the verse says the servants are given the elevated rooms, and the commentators explain the meaning: the upper rooms are the reward of the perseverance, and the verse ties the rooms to the patience, and the tying is the verse's first stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's teaching on the reward, and the teaching is that the servants' list of qualities is crowned with the elevated rooms, and the verse then states the greeting: \"and will be received with salutations and ˹greetings of˺ peace\", and the word \"salutations\" is 'tahiyyah,' and the word \"peace\" is 'salam,' and the verse says the servants are met with the greeting and the peace, and the commentators explain the meaning: the greeting of the angels is the reward's first voice, and the voice is the verse's second stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's teaching on the welcome, and the teaching is that the patient ones are welcomed with the peace, and the same welcome is described elsewhere: \"Peace be upon you for your perseverance. How excellent is the ultimate abode!\" and the verse of the chapter keeps the welcome, and the keeping is the chapter's design, and the verse then closes, and the closing is the verse's last stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's design, and the design is the next verse's subject, and the subject is the staying, and the staying is the chapter's next teaching.",
  "76": "\"staying there forever. What an excellent place to settle and reside!\" completes the reward, and the verse is the teaching on the staying, and the teaching is the verse's content. The verse states the staying: \"staying there forever. What an excellent place to settle and reside!\" and the word \"forever\" is 'khalidin,' and the verse says the staying is the permanent, and the commentators explain the meaning: the verse echoes the words of the servants' own prayer, where they called the Hell the evil place to settle and reside, and the echo is the verse's first stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's teaching on the answer, and the teaching is that the servants feared the evil place and are given the excellent place, and the verse then closes, and the closing is the verse's second stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's design, and the design is the next verse's subject, and the subject is the closing word, and the closing word is the chapter's next teaching, and the verse is the chapter's word on the staying, and the word is the verse's last stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's teaching on the two places, and the teaching is that the fear of the one and the hope of the other are the servant's two wings, and the wings are the chapter's word. The verse is the chapter's closing of the reward, and the closing is the echo, and the echo is the chapter's teaching on the answer, and the teaching is that the servants who feared the evil place are given the excellent place, and the giving is the chapter's answer to the prayer of the servants, and the answer is the chapter's final word on the list, and the word is the chapter's next verse's subject, and the subject is the chapter's closing, and the closing is the chapter's word.",
  "77": "\"Say, ˹O Prophet,˺ \"You ˹all˺ would not ˹even˺ matter to my Lord were it not for your faith ˹in Him˺. But now you ˹disbelievers˺ have denied ˹the truth˺, so the torment is bound to come\"\" closes the chapter, and the verse is the teaching on the worth, and the teaching is the verse's content. The verse quotes the closing: \"Say, ˹O Prophet,˺ \"You ˹all˺ would not ˹even˺ matter to my Lord were it not for your faith ˹in Him˺\",\" and the word \"matter\" is 'ya'ba'u,' and the verse says the worth of the people with God is the faith, and the commentators explain the meaning: the value of the human being is not the wealth or the numbers but the belief, and the saying is the verse's first stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's teaching on the worth, and the teaching is that the one without the faith has no weight in the scale of God, and the verse then states the verdict: \"But now you ˹disbelievers˺ have denied ˹the truth˺, so the torment is bound to come\", and the word \"bound\" is 'laziman,' and the verse says the denial has made the torment the attached, and the commentators explain the meaning: the denial is the earning of the torment, and the saying is the verse's second stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's teaching on the end, and the teaching is that the chapter that described the servants closes with the deniers' verdict, and the verse then closes, and the closing is the verse's last stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's design, and the design is the contrast, and the contrast is the chapter's word, and the word is the chapter's final teaching, and the teaching is that the chapter of the Standard ends where it began, with the difference between the servant and the denier, and the difference is the faith, and the faith is the chapter's word."
};
